+{"version":1,"locale":"en","articles":[{"path":"/en/articles/2026-06-20-discount-gift-cards.html","title":"Google Play Gift Card discounts: what to verify before buying","description":"Compare Google Play gift card offers by effective cost, authorized seller, US account country and currency, reward caps, and the amount you already plan to use.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Google Play Gift Card discounts: what to verify before buying","text":"There is no single retailer that is always the cheapest. Start with the amount you already expect to spend on Google Play, then compare live offers from authorized sellers by effective cost, reward cap, expiration, and the country and currency attached to the card."},{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"1. Compare effective cost, not the headline rate","text":"Effective cost is the amount paid plus required fees or extra purchases, minus only the discount or reward you can actually use. A “10% back” headline may apply to only part of the purchase when the reward cap is low. Card value: the Play balance added after redemption Amount paid: the checkout total after an immediate discount Usable reward: the part of cashback or points you can use before it expires Extra cost: fees or unrelated purchases required to qualify"},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"2. Check authorized seller, country, and currency first","text":"Google says Play gift cards should come from authorized retail partners and may be redeemed and used only in the country and currency where they were purchased. The Play country on the Google account must match the country where the code is offered. For a US account, verify that the offer is for a US Google Play gift card in USD and that the seller shown at checkout is an authorized source. A lower price does not help when the code cannot be redeemed. Google Play Help: gift cards, authorized retailers, and regional restrictions"},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"3. Read a live offer in this order","text":"Confirm the exact card country, currency, denomination, and seller. Check the offer period, minimum purchase, payment method, enrollment requirement, and reward cap. Separate the amount covered by the cap from the rest of the purchase. Count a delayed reward only when you expect to use it before expiration. Save the terms and receipt until every promised reward has posted. Retailer inventory and payment-card promotions change. Use the conditions shown on the day you buy instead of treating an old store recommendation as permanent."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"4. Buying a gift card and earning Play Points are separate","text":"Buying or redeeming a Google Play gift card is not, by itself, a normal Play Points-earning purchase. After joining Play Points, eligible Google Play purchases made from the enrolled account can earn points under the regional rules. Check the Play Points history after using the balance for an eligible app, game, in-app purchase, subscription, or other supported content. Do not add ordinary Play Points to the value of the gift card at the time the code is redeemed."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"5. Match the denomination to your plan and the reward cap","text":"Google Play balance is generally not transferable, and gift cards are usually non-refundable. Choose a denomination that fits both the purchases you expect to make and the part of the offer that earns the advertised reward."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Example","text":"If you plan to spend $80 but an offer rewards only the first $50, compare the $50 promotional portion and the remaining $30 separately. This gives a clearer answer than applying the headline rate to the full $80."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"6. Avoid unofficial codes and gift-card payment requests","text":"Never send a redemption code to someone asking for payment by gift card. Do not share your Google password or verification code with a seller. Avoid listings that do not clearly show the issuing country, currency, and seller. Keep the receipt and code private; contact the retailer and Google through official support routes if redemption fails."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Does the highest advertised discount always save the most?","text":"No. Compare the final amount paid after caps, fees, exclusions, expiration, and any extra required purchase."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Do I earn Play Points when I redeem a gift card?","text":"Redeeming the card alone is not a normal points-earning purchase. Eligible purchases made after joining Play Points can earn points under the account's US rules."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Can I use a cheaper card issued for another country?","text":"Google says the card country and currency must match the Play country and the country where the code is offered."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Which retailer should I use?","text":"Use Google's current authorized-retailer guidance, then compare the live checkout terms. A permanent ranking would become stale as inventory and payment promotions change."},{"id":"article-section-15","heading":"Match the card value to a planned purchase","text":"Estimate the purchase amount and level progress first, then compare gift-card offers only within that range. Open calculator"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/arknights-monthly-card-limited-headhunting.html","title":"Arknights Monthly Card and 300-roll Limited Headhunting planning","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. An April 2026 Global notice says the featured limited Operator is awarded after 300 rolls once, while the Data Contract Shop also shows some older","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Arknights Monthly Card and 300-roll Limited Headhunting planning","text":"An April 2026 Global notice says the featured limited Operator is awarded after 300 rolls once, while the Data Contract Shop also shows some older limited Operators at 200 contracts. The banner notice, not a static old table, is the source of truth. Which route makes sense? Separate a current 300-roll featured guarantee from Data Contract exchange targets."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Treat Monthly Card value as gradual resources, not instant 300-roll funding. Play Points come from paid Google Play transactions, not spending Orundum."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Check the exact banner series, dates and 200/300-contract target. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Is every limited Operator always 300 contracts?","text":"No. Current official notices show some older limited Operators at 200."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/colorful-stage-google-play-vs-web-store.html","title":"COLORFUL STAGE! Google Play vs Web Store: Crystals and Play Points","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. The global official site launched a Web Store in July 2025 and says it can provide better Crystal deals than the app, while warning that the store","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"COLORFUL STAGE! Google Play vs Web Store: Crystals and Play Points","text":"The global official site launched a Web Store in July 2025 and says it can provide better Crystal deals than the app, while warning that the store is unavailable in some countries and regions. Which route makes sense? If available, compare the Web Store Crystal amount with Play Points from Google Play."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"The official Web Store is an external checkout, so do not stack Play Points automatically. Compare the exact current SKU because passes and limited packs change."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Verify country availability and the Player ID before purchase. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Is the Web Store available everywhere?","text":"No. The official announcement explicitly says some countries and regions are unsupported."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/dokkan-google-play-vs-web-store.html","title":"Dokkan Battle Google Play vs official Web Store: Dragon Stones and rewards","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. Bandai Namco operates an official Dokkan Battle Web Store in supported markets. The Web Store can have different Dragon Stone bundles and its own","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Dokkan Battle Google Play vs official Web Store: Dragon Stones and rewards","text":"Bandai Namco operates an official Dokkan Battle Web Store in supported markets. The Web Store can have different Dragon Stone bundles and its own rewards; that purchase route is separate from Google Play billing. Which route makes sense? Compare the live Dragon Stone count, not only the price."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Use Play Points only on eligible Google Play billing. Never add Web Store rewards and Google Play Points together unless checkout explicitly uses Google Play."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Confirm country and currency support in the official Web Store. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Does the Dokkan Web Store automatically give Google Play Points?","text":"No. It is a separate publisher purchase route."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/efootball-google-play-points-vs-efootball-points.html","title":"Google Play Points vs eFootball Points: two completely different currencies","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. KONAMI defines eFootball Points as the eFootball Point Program currency earned through eFootball activities/campaigns and redeemed for in-game ite","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Google Play Points vs eFootball Points: two completely different currencies","text":"KONAMI defines eFootball Points as the eFootball Point Program currency earned through eFootball activities/campaigns and redeemed for in-game items after linking a KONAMI ID. eFootball Coins are another separate virtual currency. Which route makes sense? Eligible Google Play purchases of eFootball Coins can earn Google Play Points."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Google Play Points do not convert into eFootball Points. KONAMI says claimed eFootball Points expire at the end of the month six months after claim."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Keep Google Play Points, eFootball Points and eFootball Coins as three separate balances. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Can I convert Google Play Points directly to eFootball Points?","text":"No. They are separate reward systems issued by different companies."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/fgo-guaranteed-summon-play-points.html","title":"FGO 330-Summon Guarantee: Saint Quartz Cost and Google Play Points (2026)","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. FGO USA officially removed the one-time limit from the Guaranteed Summon effect on July 4, 2026; for eligible Limited Time Summons it now activate","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"FGO 330-Summon Guarantee: Saint Quartz Cost and Google Play Points (2026)","text":"FGO USA officially removed the one-time limit from the Guaranteed Summon effect on July 4, 2026; for eligible Limited Time Summons it now activates every 330 Summons. This is a server-specific rule, so another regional version must use its own current banner notice. Which route makes sense? Read the current banner before using 330 as the target."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"For Android purchases, calculate Play Points from the actual Google Play checkout amount. Keep paid-only Guaranteed Summon campaigns separate from the normal 330-Summon safety rule."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Do not convert Japanese pack prices into a US price table; use the live US Saint Quartz packs. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Does every FGO server already use the same 330 rule?","text":"No. Server schedules can differ; use the current official notice for the version you play."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/genshin-welkin-moon-play-points.html","title":"Blessing of the Welkin Moon: Value, Genesis Crystals and Play Points","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. Welkin is a time-based product: much of its value arrives through daily Primogem claims, so it should not be compared with an immediate Genesis Cr","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Blessing of the Welkin Moon: Value, Genesis Crystals and Play Points","text":"Welkin is a time-based product: much of its value arrives through daily Primogem claims, so it should not be compared with an immediate Genesis Crystal top-up only by sticker price. Which route makes sense? Value only the daily Primogems you expect to claim."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Compare direct Genesis Crystal top-ups separately when you need currency now. An official HoYoverse web checkout is not automatically a Google Play transaction."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Use the live US price shown in the game or official checkout; prices and payment routes can change. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Does an official HoYoverse website purchase automatically earn Play Points?","text":"No. Play Points require an eligible Google Play transaction."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-balance-combine-payment.html","title":"Can you combine Google Play balance with another payment method?","description":"Learn when an insufficient Google Play balance can be combined with another payment method, how Play Points, Play credit and coupons differ, and what to check at checkout.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: often yes when the balance is insufficient","text":"Google’s payment help says that when your Google Play balance is lower than the purchase amount, you can use another payment method or a promotion for the remaining amount. The option still depends on the item, country, account, tax-inclusive total and choices shown on the final checkout screen. Play balance and Play Points are different. In eligible countries, Google also supports applying points rewards at checkout with another payment method. Use that option only when your account actually shows it; you do not always have to exchange points for credit first."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"First separate points, credit, balance and coupons","text":"Play Points are reward points. You redeem them for an item, coupon, partner reward or Google Play credit. Play credit becomes part of your Google Play balance after redemption. A coupon has its own eligible app, minimum purchase and combination rules. If you are still on the Play Points “Use” screen, you have not yet created a general payment balance. Check the reward details before redeeming because exchanged credit and coupons can expire."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"How to check a split payment safely","text":"Open the product with the Google account that owns the balance. Continue to the purchase confirmation screen. Confirm the Google Play balance amount and the full price including tax. Look for a second payment method for the remaining amount. Do not complete the purchase if the expected balance, coupon or final total is missing. The checkout screen is more reliable than a generic example because Google can restrict payment combinations by product, country and account."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Common reasons the second payment option does not appear","text":"The balance is on a different Google account from the account making the purchase. The balance currency or Play country does not match the transaction. The item cannot be purchased with Google Play balance. A coupon or promotion allows only one credit or promotion at a time. The balance covers the pre-tax price but not the tax-inclusive total, or the checkout has a region-specific restriction. Google Play balance also has product limits. It cannot be used as a general wallet for hardware, cash transfer, gift-card purchases or Google Pay top-ups."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"When Play credit is a better choice than a coupon","text":"Play credit is usually more flexible when you have not chosen a specific app yet. A coupon may offer better value for one app, but only when you will meet its minimum purchase and use it before expiry. Compare the actual checkout total, not only the apparent discount percentage. After redeeming Points, keep a screenshot or note of the reward name and expiry date. If a payment fails, the return of balance or points may not be instant, so verify the order status before retrying repeatedly."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Can you combine Google Play balance with another payment method?","text":"Yes. If your Play balance does not cover the full amount, you can pay the remainder with another supported payment method at checkout."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Why does the split payment option not show up?","text":"Split payments may be restricted for subscriptions, certain in-app items, or when accounts or currencies do not match."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-games-vs-play-points.html","title":"Google Play Games XP is not the same as Google Play Points","description":"Understand the difference between a Play Games profile and Google Play Points, when playing or buying can earn rewards, and how quests and PC purchases fit together.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: Play Games XP and Play Points are separate systems","text":"A Play Games profile stores a gamer name, achievements, game statistics, progress and XP. Google Play Points is a separate rewards program with a spendable points balance, level progress, coupons and other benefits. Playing a game, earning an achievement or raising Play Games XP does not automatically add Play Points. Points come from eligible Google Play purchases or a specific quest, install offer or reward shown to your account."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"What the Play Games profile does","text":"Your Play Games profile is a gaming identity used across supported Android games and Google Play Games on PC. It can track achievements, streaks, statistics, saved progress and community activity. Its XP or profile level measures game-profile activity. It is not money, cannot be exchanged for Play credit and does not move your Play Points status."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"What Play Points does","text":"Play Points rewards eligible Google Play purchases and selected promotions. The program has a spendable balance and a separate calendar-year level progress counter. In the United States, the Bronze base rate is 1 point per $1 before tax, with higher rates at Silver, Gold, Platinum and Diamond. These rates are unrelated to Play Games XP."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"When playing can still matter","text":"A quest may ask you to install, play, sign in with Play Games or complete other actions. If that quest is shown to your account and you complete its listed steps, the quest can award Play Points. The important distinction is that the offer creates the reward. Ordinary play time or an achievement outside an offer does not guarantee points."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Purchases on Google Play Games on PC","text":"Google says eligible Google Play purchases can earn points on computer as well as mobile. Google Play Games on PC can synchronize progress and support Play Points on eligible transactions. However, some features are platform-specific. Google’s troubleshooting guidance says quests and stamp cards are currently available on mobile, not on Google Play Games on PC or the web. Check the phone app when an offer is missing."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Account mismatches are easy to miss","text":"A game may use one Play Games profile while the Play Store purchase is made with another Google Account. That can split game progress, receipts and Play Points records. Before troubleshooting, compare the account shown in Play Games, the Google Play payment sheet and the Play Points page. Points cannot be transferred between accounts."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Use the right screen for the right question","text":"Achievements, saved progress and gamer XP: open the Play Games profile. Purchase points and redemptions: open Play Points and Points history. Quest progress: open the quest page in the mobile Play Store. Order details: open Google Play payments and subscriptions. Keeping these screens separate prevents a Play Games level from being mistaken for a Play Points level."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Is Google Play Games XP the same as Play Points?","text":"No. Play Games XP is for gamer profile leveling and cannot be redeemed for discounts or items, unlike Play Points."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-100-value.html","title":"How much are 100 Google Play Points worth in the US?","description":"US account example: estimate spending needed for 100 Google Play Points by US level, then see why redemption value varies by reward, country, and account.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"calculator_bridge","sections":[{"id":"reading-answer","heading":"US estimate: earning 100 points is not the same as redeeming them","text":"At the US Bronze rate, 100 points take about $100 of eligible pre-tax spending before promotions and purchase-level rounding. That is an earning-cost estimate, not $100 of redeemable value. The rewards shown in your Use tab determine what the points can buy. This guide uses United States earning rates. Outside the US, use the method with your own Play country’s currency and rate rather than converting the dollar amount."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Compare the rewards before exchanging","text":"Suppose your account offers either a $1 credit or a $2 coupon for 100 points. Those are hypothetical offers, not a current US exchange rate. If you already plan an eligible purchase and meet the coupon minimum, the $2 discount may save more. If using it requires an extra purchase you did not want, the flexible credit may have more value to you. 100 points are not cash. Their use value depends on rewards actually shown on the account."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Spending needed at each US level","text":"Using the cited US base rates, the rough eligible spend for 100 points is: Bronze $100 at 1 point per $1; Silver about $90.91 at 1.1; Gold about $83.34 at 1.2; Platinum about $71.43 at 1.4; Diamond $62.50 at 1.6. Google calculates each eligible item and rounds to the nearest whole point. Taxes are excluded, so several purchases can differ from one division of the total."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Why redemption value is not fixed","text":"Play Points can be used for in-app items, coupons, Play credit, or partner offers when available. Catalogs and required points differ by country, time, and account. Check the Use tab, restrictions, minimum purchase, and expiration before treating a reward as savings."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"How US promotions change the estimate","text":"At US Bronze, an eligible 2x offer can reduce the rough spend from $100 to $50, and a 3x offer to about $33.34. That calculation applies only when the specific transaction qualifies. Start at the normal rate, then check activation, dates, apps, caps, and account eligibility."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"What non-US users should do","text":"Do not convert the dollar table with an exchange rate and assume the result is a local Play Points requirement. Countries can have different thresholds, earning rates, taxes, rewards, and promotions. Use the general points-cost calculator with the values shown on the purchasing account."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Calculate with your own account values","text":"US users can use the cited rates; other users should replace them with local Google Play values. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Are 100 points always worth $1?","text":"No. There is no universal cash rate."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"How much do I spend for 100 points in the US?","text":"About $100 at US Bronze before promotions and excluding tax."},{"id":"article-section-14","heading":"Can I use this table outside the US?","text":"Use only the calculation method and enter the local account values."},{"id":"article-section-15","heading":"Can points be cashed out?","text":"No."},{"id":"article-section-16","heading":"Next step","text":"Points-to-cost calculator General level guide Country differences Editorial policy"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-500-1000-cost.html","title":"How much spending is needed for 500 or 1,000 Google Play Points?","description":"Compare fixed-rate estimates for 500 or 1,000 US Play Points with staged level progression. Check pre-tax prices, purchase rounding and the rate actually shown at checkout.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"calculator_bridge","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: distinguish a fixed-rate comparison from a level-up estimate","text":"At a fixed rate of 1 point per $1, 500 points correspond to $500 and 1,000 to $1,000 before tax. These are fixed-rate references: if purchases raise your level along the way, later purchases can use a higher rate. These are planning estimates. Google rounds points for individual purchases, and promotions or ineligible items can change the final result."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"US level-by-level estimate","text":"Level Base earn rate About 500 points About 1,000 points Bronze 1 pt / $1 $500 $1,000 Silver 1.1 pt / $1 $454.55 $909.09 Gold 1.2 pt / $1 $416.67 $833.33 Platinum 1.4 pt / $1 $357.14 $714.29 Diamond 1.6 pt / $1 $312.5 $625 Every row holds the stated rate constant. From zero, a continuous estimate for 500 points is $150 at Bronze plus 350 ÷ 1.1 at Silver, or about $468.18. Actual purchase sizes, rounding and when the higher rate starts can change that amount. A single purchase is not assumed to switch rates partway through."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Why the number is not a guaranteed bill total","text":"Google calculates points from each eligible item price, applies the level or promotion rate and rounds to a whole point. Without knowing the exact purchase sizes, a table cannot reproduce every rounding result. Use the table for scale, then use the calculator’s average purchase amount option or compare the final checkout estimate."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"500 points can mean balance or level progress","text":"If you want 500 spendable points, check your current balance first. If you want 500 more points toward a level, check the remaining annual progress instead. Redeeming points reduces the balance but not the progress already earned that year. Do not subtract one counter from the other."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Promotions can reduce required spending","text":"A 2x or 3x offer can lower the eligible spending needed, but only for purchases that satisfy the offer and only during its active period. Some offers require activation before purchase. Compare the current tier's normal earn-rate path first. A promotion is not a saving if it causes an unplanned purchase."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Use the Play country of the purchasing account","text":"This page uses US dollars and US thresholds. Do not relabel a dollar result as pounds or euros. Select the rules for the purchasing account’s Play country, even when you read the guide in English. Country-specific content availability and benefits can still differ, so this article’s dollar examples are specifically US examples."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"How to get a closer estimate","text":"Check the exact points still needed in Google Play. Select the current US level and any confirmed promotion rate. Enter an average amount per purchase if you know it. Exclude tax from manual calculations. Confirm the actual award in Points history after purchase. Never spend only to make the estimate “come out even.”"},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"How much do I need to spend for 1,000 Play Points?","text":"At a constant 1 point per $1 the reference is $1,000 before tax. Starting from zero and progressing through levels gives a different estimate; use current annual progress and the rate shown before each purchase."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-apps-books-purchases.html","title":"Paid apps, games, subscriptions and books can earn Play Points—but eligibility still matters","description":"See which Google Play purchases can earn Play Points in the United States, including paid apps, games, in-app purchases, subscriptions and books, plus tax and account checks.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: eligible Google Play purchases can earn Play Points","text":"For a United States Play Points account, Google lists paid apps and games, in-app or in-game purchases, subscriptions and books as ways to earn points. The purchase must be made through Google Play with the enrolled Google Account. Taxes do not earn points, and the content available for purchase or point earning can depend on country. Check the checkout estimate and Points history for the final result."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"Paid apps and complete games","text":"Buying a paid app or game from Google Play can earn Play Points when the item is eligible and the purchase uses the account enrolled in Play Points. This is different from downloading a free app: a normal free download does not automatically create points. A free app or game can award points only when Google shows a specific install promotion or quest. The offer page, not the app’s normal store listing, defines the requirement."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"In-app purchases and subscriptions","text":"Purchases processed through Google Play inside an app or game can earn points, including eligible recurring subscriptions. An external website checkout or another billing system is not the same as a Google Play transaction. Before paying, confirm the Google Play payment sheet, the purchasing Google Account and any special earn-rate offer. After payment, use Points history to verify the actual award."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Books are a real US category","text":"Do not assume every book has the same promotion. Monthly or account-specific multipliers appear in the Earn area when available, and the item page or checkout remains the practical check."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Tax, discounts and rounding","text":"Google calculates points from the eligible item price and excludes taxes. The result is multiplied by the earning rate and rounded to the nearest whole point. A discounted checkout can therefore differ from a calculation based on the original list price. For small purchases, purchase-by-purchase rounding can also make a monthly total estimate differ from Points history."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Purchases on other devices","text":"Eligible Google Play purchases can earn points from Android devices, computers and Smart TVs. Google Play Games on PC can also support eligible purchases, but the account and Google Play billing path still matter. Playing a game or earning Play Games XP is not itself the same as earning Play Points. Look for an eligible purchase, install promotion or quest."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Pre-purchase checklist","text":"Use the Google Account enrolled in US Play Points. Confirm the payment is processed by Google Play. Read any promotion terms before activating or purchasing. Do not include sales tax in a manual point estimate. Check the estimate at checkout and the completed entry in Points history. If the purchase category is available but no points appear, verify enrollment date, order status and account before contacting support."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Do book purchases earn Play Points?","text":"Yes. Purchases of eligible paid apps, games, books, and in-app items earn Play Points."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-balance-history-progress.html","title":"Google Play Points balance, history and level progress are three different numbers","description":"Learn the difference between your spendable Play Points balance, transaction history and calendar-year level progress, with US thresholds and the exact menus to check.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: check the balance, points history and level progress separately","text":"Your points balance is what you can currently use. Points history explains where points were earned, used or reversed. Level progress counts eligible points earned during the calendar year and is not reduced when you spend points. A number can therefore look “wrong” only because you are comparing two different counters. Start by identifying which counter you actually need."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"What each number means","text":"Points balance Spendable points available for Play credit, coupons or participating items. Using points reduces this balance. Points history A transaction record for earned points, weekly prizes, redemptions and reversals after refunds. Level progress Points earned in the current calendar year toward the next level. Redeeming points does not remove them from this progress. This separation matters most near a level threshold: a person can have a low spendable balance after redeeming points while still keeping a much higher annual progress total."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Where to check in the Play Store app","text":"Open Google Play Store with the enrolled Google Account. Tap the profile picture, then Play Points . Open Use to see the current balance and expiration information. Open the menu and choose Points history to inspect individual entries. Open level benefits or the progress panel to see the current level, base earning rate and points needed for the next level. If several Google Accounts are on the device, verify the account icon before comparing any totals. Play Points cannot be combined across accounts."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"US level progress uses US thresholds","text":"For a United States account, the official thresholds are 150 points for Silver, 600 for Gold, 3,000 for Platinum and 10,000 for Diamond. The Bronze base rate is 1 point per $1 of eligible item price, before tax. Do not compare a US account with Japan, Korea or Taiwan screenshots. Their earning units and some thresholds are different even when the app layout looks similar."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Why a refund or cancellation changes two places","text":"When a purchase that earned points is returned or cancelled, Google deducts the related points from both the spendable balance and level progress. The history entry is therefore the best place to identify a sudden decrease. If the order is still pending, or the purchase came from another account, the balance and annual progress may not update as expected. Check the order account and purchase status before assuming a calculation error."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Expiration and level retention are different clocks","text":"Spendable points expire one year after the last time you earned or used points. Level progress, by contrast, is evaluated by calendar year. After reaching a level, you generally keep it through the end of the next calendar year, then the following year’s level is based on points earned during the prior year. That means the balance expiration date, the current-year progress total and the date your level may change can all be different."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"A quick mismatch checklist","text":"Confirm the same Google Account was used for the purchase and for Play Points. Compare the completed purchase with the matching entry in Points history. Separate spendable balance from annual level progress. Check whether points were used, expired or reversed after a refund. Use the US thresholds only for a US-profile account. If the history entry itself is missing after the purchase is complete, keep the order ID and contact Google Play support rather than manually adding an estimated amount."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Where can I see my Play Points history?","text":"Open Google Play, tap your profile icon, select Play Points, and navigate to the More → Points history."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-cash-conversion.html","title":"Can You Redeem Google Play Points for Cash?","description":"Can you redeem Google Play Points for cash? No—but Play credit, coupons, and eligible rewards can still reduce planned Google Play spending. See the rules.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Can You Redeem Google Play Points for Cash?","text":"No. You cannot redeem Google Play Points for cash, cash them out to a bank or wallet, or transfer them to another Google account. But that does not make the points useless. If you already plan to spend on Google Play, eligible Play credit, coupons, partner vouchers, or in-app and in-game rewards can reduce spending you were already going to make . Start by checking what your account actually shows under Play Points → Use, then compare the reward with what you would otherwise pay. ( Google’s official guidance )"},{"id":"answer","heading":"What you can and cannot do","text":"Action Available? What to know Redeem or convert points for cash No Google explicitly says points cannot be converted to cash Withdraw to a bank or wallet No No official cash-out or withdrawal feature exists Transfer to another account or family member No Points cannot be transferred between accounts or family members Redeem eligible Play rewards When shown Play credit, coupons, items, vouchers, and other rewards vary by account and market “Redeemable” does not mean “cashable.” A reward that works inside Google Play does not become unrestricted money."},{"id":"alternatives","heading":"What to do instead of cashing out","text":"You cannot turn Play Points into unrestricted cash, but you may be able to use them to offset a Google Play purchase you already planned to make. Google Play credit: if it appears in your Use tab, you can redeem points for credit toward eligible Google Play purchases. Google says Play credit received from points expires after one year. Coupons: choose them when the eligible app or game, minimum purchase, and discount fit a purchase you actually intend to make. Coupons redeemed with points expire after one year. In-app or in-game items and partner vouchers: when offered, compare the required points with the price or saving you would otherwise pay for. The more useful question is not “What cash rate can I get?” but “Can these points replace money I was already going to spend?” See how Play Points rewards, coupons, and Play credit work , then compare the practical value of 100 Play Points ."},{"id":"credit","heading":"Why Play credit is not cash","text":"Google Play credit is a balance for eligible purchases on Google Play. It is not a checking balance, prepaid debit card, or general-purpose wallet, and it is not a route to a bank withdrawal. Google says Play credit received by exchanging points expires after one year. Coupons exchanged with points also expire after one year and can have app or game restrictions and minimum-purchase requirements. Redeem only when the reward fits something you expect to use."},{"id":"check","heading":"How to check your real options","text":"Open Google Play with the account enrolled in Play Points. Select your profile, then Play Points. Open Use and review the rewards currently shown for your account. Check the required points, eligible app or game, minimum purchase, expiration, and any other displayed conditions. Redeem only when the reward fits a purchase you already plan to make. Rewards vary by country, account, and time. Treat the current Play Store screen for your account as the deciding source."},{"id":"value","heading":"Earning cost vs redemption value","text":"The spend required to earn 100 points can be estimated from your level and a verified promotion. What those 100 points can unlock depends on the selected reward. There is no universal cash exchange rate that makes those two numbers equal. ( Google’s official guidance ) As a hypothetical US example, suppose a reward lets you reduce an eligible $6 purchase to $3. You have avoided $3 of planned spending; you have not created $3 that you can withdraw or send to someone. If you did not want the purchase in the first place, the remaining $3 is still new spending. This example does not state a current redemption rate. So the fact that Play Points cannot be cashed out does not make their use value zero, and it also does not justify assigning every point one fixed cash value. Compare the benefit of a reward you would actually use with the points it costs. Your spendable point balance and annual level progress are also separate. Redeeming points reduces the balance, but does not by itself undo the points counted toward your current-year level progress."},{"id":"safety","heading":"Avoid cash-out scams","text":"A site or person offering a secret cash-out method is not using an official Play Points feature. Do not share passwords, verification codes, gift codes, identity documents, or remote access to your device. Account sales and off-platform trades can create risks such as nonpayment, account takeover, and personal-data exposure. If a path is outside the redemption options shown by Google Play, do not assume it is a supported cash-out method; prioritize account security."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"What is the closest practical alternative to cashing out Play Points?","text":"If you already plan to spend on Google Play, use eligible Play credit, coupons, partner vouchers, or in-app and in-game rewards shown in your account to reduce that planned spending."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Can I gift my points?","text":"No. Google does not provide an account-to-account or family transfer feature."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Can a points redemption be refunded?","text":"It depends on the reward. Google says in-app or in-game items and donations redeemed with points cannot be refunded. Check the displayed terms for coupons, Play credit, and other rewards before redeeming."},{"id":"article-section-14","heading":"Is one point worth a fixed amount?","text":"No. There is no universal cash rate. Compare the points required with the reward you would actually use."},{"id":"article-section-15","heading":"If cash-out is not available, check these next","text":"How Play Points rewards, coupons, and Play credit work How much are 100 Play Points worth to use? What to check when Play credit cannot be exchanged or used"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-country-change.html","title":"Change Google Play country: what happens to US Play Points?","description":"Learn the location, local payment method, 90-day, family, balance and Play Points consequences of changing a Google Play country to or from the United States.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Change Google Play country: what happens to US Play Points?","text":"Changing Play country is not a language switch. Google requires you to be in the new country and have a payment method from that country. Play Points and level do not transfer, and the old Play balance remains tied to the old country."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Requirements before changing country","text":"You must be physically in the new country and add a payment method issued for that country. Google also applies a 90-day interval after setting or changing the Play country. Members of a Google family group cannot change Play country until they leave the group. The change can take up to 48 hours."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Play Points and level are lost","text":"When the Play country changes, the existing Play Points balance and level do not move to the new country. Joining again starts under the new country's thresholds and earning unit. Moving from the US to Korea or Taiwan does not convert 150 US Silver points into the local Silver progress. The new account view follows the destination rules."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Play balance, apps and subscriptions","text":"The old Google Play balance stays associated with the old country and cannot be used in the new country. Some apps, books, movies and other content may no longer be available. Existing subscriptions can continue, require a payment update or behave differently depending on the provider and country. Review important subscriptions before changing."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Checklist before confirming","text":"Record your points, level, old balance, family status and active subscriptions. Confirm the destination country is the place where you now live and that its payment method is ready. After the change, wait up to 48 hours, then verify the Play country and rejoin Play Points if the program is available."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Calculate with your regional settings","text":"Enter the remaining points and the confirmed earning rate shown on your account. Open the US calculator"},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Can I keep US Play Points after changing country?","text":"No. Points and level do not transfer to the new Play country."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Can I use my old US Play balance in the new country?","text":"No. It remains tied to the old country."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Can I change country while in a family group?","text":"No. Leave the family group first, subject to Google's family rules."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-country-differences.html","title":"Google Play Points rules differ by Play country","description":"Compare official Play Points earning units and level thresholds for the US, Korea, Taiwan, Hong Kong, India, and Japan, with links to the matching calculator or local guide.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Google Play Points rules differ by Play country","text":"The phone language does not choose the Play Points rules. The relevant region is the Google Play country attached to the purchasing account. That country can change earning units, level thresholds, reward catalogs, promotions, payment methods, and available benefits."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Quick comparison of official level systems","text":"Play country Base unit at Bronze Platinum starts Diamond starts United States 1 point per $1 3,000 points 10,000 points South Korea 1 point per ₩1,000 2,400 points 15,000 points Taiwan 1 point per NT$30 4,000 points 15,000 points Hong Kong 1 point per HK$7 4,000 points 15,000 points India 1 point per ₹5 4,000 points No Diamond tier Japan 1 point per ¥100 4,000 points 15,000 points This table is a navigation aid, not a substitute for the full local level table. Silver and Gold thresholds, higher-level rates, weekly rewards, and account offers also differ. India currently tops out at Platinum, so it has no Diamond threshold."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Choose the guide that matches your question","text":"Your question Open this guide What are the levels and rates for my account? US levels and benefits , Korea , or Taiwan I use a Hong Kong or India Play account. Hong Kong calculator or India calculator What happens if I change the Play country? Country-change effects and pre-change checklist How much might Platinum or Diamond require? US Platinum 3,000 vs Diamond 10,000 Can a promotion from another country apply? Promotion eligibility and account checks Can I use a gift card from another region? Gift-card country and currency checks"},{"id":"section-3","heading":"What can be compared across countries","text":"The calculation method is portable: start with missing annual points, the current local earning rate, and an offer that is actually displayed. The currency amount and thresholds are not portable. A screenshot from another country can show where to look, but it cannot prove that the same reward, special earn rate, or redemption option exists for your account."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Before using a calculator","text":"Open the Google account that will make the purchase. Confirm its Play country and currency. Read the level and rate shown in Play Points. Use only promotions displayed for that account. Check Points history after the purchase."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Does changing the phone language change the Play country?","text":"No. The purchasing account’s Google Play country and payment profile determine the regional rules."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Can I use another country’s threshold or promotion?","text":"No. Use another region only to learn where to look; calculate with the thresholds, currency and offers shown for the purchasing account."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"What should I check before changing the Play country?","text":"Review the country-change guide first because points, subscriptions, balance, payment methods and available content can be affected."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Calculate with local account values","text":"Enter the missing points and earning rate shown for the purchasing account rather than copying another country's currency example. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Next step","text":"All six calculator regions US levels guide Country-change guide US upper-level cost guide Editorial policy"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-coupon-not-applied.html","title":"Google Play Points coupon not applied: checks that fix it","description":"Fix a Google Play Points coupon that does not apply by checking the app, purchase value, competing promotion, account, country, and currency.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"troubleshooting","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Google Play Points coupon not applied: checks that fix it","text":"A saved coupon does not guarantee that every checkout qualifies. It will not apply when the app, purchase value, account, country, currency, or competing promotion is outside the coupon conditions. Compare the saved offer and checkout before buying again."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Confirm the coupon under Saved offers","text":"Open your profile → Play Points → More → Points history to check the redemption. Google’s learning guide also lists Notifications & offers → Saved offers. If one route does not show the coupon, check the other and confirm that you are using the account that redeemed it. Google Play Points / Google Play Learning Center Record the coupon name, target app, discount amount, and expiry. Keep screenshots private and do not post order numbers or payment details publicly."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"The item must be equal to or greater than the coupon","text":"Google's troubleshooting guidance says the item must be worth at least as much as the coupon. A $3 coupon, for example, cannot be applied to a $1.99 purchase. Compare the final checkout value, including regional pricing and tax handling. The product's headline price may not be the exact amount used for the eligibility check."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Only one credit or promotion can be used at a time","text":"Google says you can use only one credit or promotion at a time. If another offer is selected, the Play Points coupon may not be the automatic choice. Open the payment method or promotion row at checkout and select the saved coupon you want. Do not calculate a purchase on the assumption that two discounts will stack."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Match the app, same account, country, and currency","text":"Play Points coupons apply to qualifying in-app purchases for the specified app or game. Another app, excluded item, or web checkout may not qualify. The coupon is tied to the same account that redeemed it and to its country or region and currency. On a multi-account device or after a country change, confirm the purchasing account and Play country."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"If every visible condition matches","text":"Close and reopen the app or game, and confirm that Google Play and the target app are current. Google's troubleshooting steps also include force stopping the app and checking Play Store cache when delivery or redemption fails. If it still does not apply, save the coupon, target item, and pre-purchase screens and contact Google Play support or the app developer. Avoid a duplicate purchase just to test it."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Check the condition before spending again","text":"Identify why the reward is unavailable, then estimate only the purchase you already plan to make. Open the calculator"},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Where is my redeemed coupon?","text":"Check Play Points → More → Points history. Google also documents Notifications & offers → Saved offers; check the other route if needed."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Can I use a coupon on a cheaper item?","text":"No. The item must be equal to or greater than the coupon value."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Can I stack it with another promotion?","text":"Google says only one credit or promotion can be used at a time."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-device-change.html","title":"Google Play Points after changing phones or devices","description":"Google Play Points normally stay with your Google account after changing phones. Check the signed-in account, Play country, payment profile and point history if they are missing.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: the same Google account normally keeps your Points","text":"Google Play Points are associated with the Google account and Play country used for the program, not stored only on one phone. A normal device replacement should not erase the balance, level or point history when you sign in to the same account. When the new phone shows zero Points or no Play Points menu, first suspect an account or country mismatch rather than a lost balance."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"Choose the symptom that matches your screen","text":"The Play Points menu is missing: check the active Google account, Play country and program availability. The menu exists but the balance is zero: compare the profile email and open point history. The level looks different: confirm that you did not switch accounts or Play countries. A recent purchase is missing: check the purchase account, order status and the point history before blaming the device change. This symptom-first approach avoids signing out of every account or changing settings that are unrelated to the actual problem."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Check the account in this order","text":"Open Google Play and tap the profile image. Read the full email address of the current account. Switch to the account that originally joined Play Points. Open Play Points and then point history. Compare the purchase receipt account if only one transaction is missing. Multiple Google accounts can be present on one phone, but their Play Points cannot be combined or moved. The account that made a purchase receives the eligible Points."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Do not change the Play country as a quick fix","text":"A device replacement and a Play country change are different events. Google warns that changing the Play country can cause the previous country’s Points, level and unused rewards to be lost. It can also affect payment methods and available content. Only change the Play country when you actually meet Google’s country-change conditions and understand the consequences. It should not be the first troubleshooting step for a new phone."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"When to wait and when to contact support","text":"Some account benefits or recent transactions can take time to appear on another device. Restart Google Play, update the app and check the history again before repeating purchases. A Play Pass benefit may also have a separate confirmation period. Contact Google Play support when the same account and country show a verified completed purchase but the corresponding history remains missing. Prepare the order ID, date, amount, account email and screenshots; do not publish those details publicly."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Do Play Points transfer when switching devices?","text":"Yes. Play Points are tied to your Google account, so logging into the same account transfers your points automatically."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-discounts-promo-codes.html","title":"Discounts and promo codes can change the Play Points calculation","description":"Learn how Play Store offers, promo codes, Play Points coupons and sale prices affect a US Play Points estimate, and what to verify before and after checkout.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: do not calculate from the original list price—check the eligible checkout price","text":"Google calculates Play Points from the eligible item price and excludes tax. A sale, promo code or coupon can change what you actually pay, so points should not be estimated from the crossed-out list price without checking checkout. Offers also have account, currency, country, app and expiration conditions. The pre-purchase point estimate and completed Points history are the final practical checks."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"Four things often called a “promo”","text":"Store sale The item itself has a lower price for eligible users. Google Play promo code A code gives an offer or discount and may need to be redeemed before its expiration. Play Points coupon Points are exchanged for an app-specific discount that applies when the purchase meets its conditions. Earn-rate promotion The price may stay the same while the points-per-dollar rate increases. These mechanisms affect either the payment amount, the earning rate or both, so they should not be mixed into one shortcut."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Use the final eligible item price, not tax","text":"Google’s earning guide says points are based on the item price and do not include taxes paid. If a discount lowers the eligible item price, a calculation based on the original price can overstate the expected points. The safest approach is to look for the point estimate on the Google Play payment sheet after the discount is applied."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Play Points coupons have app and value conditions","text":"A coupon exchanged with Play Points applies automatically only to an in-app purchase that meets the coupon conditions. The item must be at least the required value, and the coupon is tied to an account, app, currency and country or region. A US-dollar coupon should not be expected to work after changing Play country or on a different Google Account."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Promo codes and Offers are account-specific","text":"Google Play promotions can differ based on account settings, activity and location. Some promo codes must be redeemed immediately, while others can be saved under Offers until their expiry date. Do not assume a code shared online is valid for your account, country or currency. Read the displayed terms before changing the cart."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"An earn-rate boost is different from a price discount","text":"A 5-points-per-$1 boost can increase points without reducing the item price. A $5 discount can reduce payment without changing the base earning rate. If both appear, confirm whether the terms allow them together. Activate an earn-rate offer before purchase when the offer requires activation. A screenshot after purchase does not retroactively change eligibility."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Checkout and history checklist","text":"Confirm the purchasing Google Account and US Play country. Apply the code or coupon and verify the final item price. Exclude sales tax from a manual estimate. Check whether a point multiplier is active and app-specific. After purchase, compare the receipt with Points history. If the discount worked but points did not match, separate the payment problem from the point-award problem before contacting support."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Do discounted purchases earn full Play Points?","text":"Use the eligible item price after discounts, excluding tax, and the rate shown for the purchase. Check the point estimate before paying."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-earn-free.html","title":"How to earn Google Play Points without adding an unplanned purchase","description":"Choose the legitimate Play Points benefit that fits your US account: weekly prizes, Super Weekly Prize, featured installs, quests, Play Pass bonuses, or promotions for a planned purchase.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: check account benefits before adding a purchase","text":"For this guide, “free” means a legitimate benefit that may be claimed without adding a new purchase to your plan. Examples can include the regular weekly prize, a featured free-app install, or a quest whose steps do not require a purchase. Purchase quests, multipliers, and Play Pass rewards are still useful, but they have a cost or subscription condition. The Earn , Perks , and Play Pass screens on your own US account determine which options are available."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"Choose by what you see now","text":"Your situation Check first Cost condition Detailed guide Silver or higher Regular weekly prize No new purchase to claim Weekly prize and Play Pass differences Gold or higher and a card is shown Super Weekly Prize No new purchase to claim; stock and extra rules may apply Super Weekly Prize and Super Tickets A featured install appears in Earn The exact app, use time, and deadline May require no paid purchase Use the terms shown on the offer A quest appears All steps before starting Some are no-purchase; others require purchases Quest eligibility and troubleshooting You already subscribe to Play Pass Thursday bonus or booster Play Pass subscription required Compare the weekly programs You already plan a purchase Multiplier, quest, and level progress Purchase required Promotion and level-rate rules"},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"1. Regular weekly prize","text":"US members at Silver or higher can claim a weekly prize from the Perks area. It resets on Friday in the Play country. Check Points history when you are unsure whether the current week was already claimed."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"2. Super Weekly Prize","text":"Super Ticket availability and the effect on an existing prize must be read on the current account card. Do not rely on an old ticket schedule."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"3. Featured app or game install","text":"Google may display an offer for installing and using a particular free app or game. It is not a general reward for every free download. Follow the deadline, first-install, use-time, and personalization conditions shown on the card."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"When a quest includes a purchase","text":"A quest can include playing, signing in, installing, subscribing, or making eligible purchases. The word “quest” does not tell you whether it is free. Read every step and the deadline before starting. Look for a minimum amount, purchase count, subscription, or eligible-title rule. When a purchase is required, compare it with a purchase you already planned. After completion, check both the quest card and Points history. Using Play Points to pay may not count toward a purchase quest, and refunds can reverse progress or rewards. The quest card shown to your account is the controlling condition."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Play Pass rewards are for people who already have the subscription","text":"Eligible US Play Pass subscribers and family members can receive a Thursday Play Points bonus or earning-rate booster. The reward is separate from the regular Friday weekly prize. If you already use Play Pass for its apps and games, remember to check the weekly benefit. If you are considering a new subscription, compare the subscription's app and game value together with the weekly reward rather than treating the points alone as a fixed allowance."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"For a planned purchase, compare the available benefits together","text":"A purchase is not a problem when it is part of your plan. Before checkout, review the Earn tab for an app-specific multiplier, a quest, the offer period, and your current level rate. Then verify the result in Points history."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Estimate points from a planned purchase","text":"Enter the level rate and multiplier displayed on your US account to compare expected points and level progress. Open the calculator"},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"How to recognize a fake “free points” offer","text":"It asks for a Google password or verification code. It promises unlimited points, cash conversion, or transfers between accounts. It requires an unofficial APK, browser extension, or remote-access app. It asks for a payment or gift-card code before releasing points. Legitimate benefits are visible in Google Play or documented by Google. A third-party site cannot directly add Play Points to your account."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"A simple weekly routine","text":"Thursday: check Play Pass rewards when you already subscribe. Friday: claim the regular weekly prize and a Super Weekly Prize if it is displayed. Before a purchase: read multipliers, install offers, and quests in Earn. After a purchase or claim: check Points history and the active Google account."},{"id":"article-section-15","heading":"Can I earn Play Points without spending money?","text":"Some account offers can award points without a new purchase. Google Opinion Rewards is different: on Android, surveys award Play credit, not Play Points. A later eligible purchase using that credit is a separate transaction. Google Opinion Rewards"},{"id":"article-section-16","heading":"Detailed guides","text":"Regular weekly prize and Play Pass weekly benefit Super Weekly Prize and Super Tickets Google Play quests not appearing or completing Promotion not applied"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-expiration.html","title":"When do Google Play Points expire?","description":"Google Play Points expire one year after the last time you earn or use points. Learn what resets the date and how to check the exact expiry shown in Play.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"When do Google Play Points expire?","text":"Google Play Points expire one year after the last time you earned or used points. The date is based on point activity, not January 1 and not your level renewal date. Check Play Points → Use for the date shown on your account."},{"id":"rule","heading":"The one-year rule","text":"The balance does not expire one year after each individual purchase. Google describes one expiry date based on your latest point activity. If eligible activity occurs before that date, the balance expiry date moves forward. Date or balance Rule Where to check Play Points balance Expires one year after the latest earning or use Play Points → Use Level progress Counted by calendar year Play Points → Benefits Redeemed coupon / Play credit Has its own expiry Reward details or payment methods"},{"id":"activity","heading":"What resets the expiry date","text":"Earning points from an eligible purchase or promotion, and using points for an eligible reward, count as point activity. Merely opening the Play Store, viewing the balance, or using Play credit without earning or spending Play Points should not be treated as a reset. Do not make an unnecessary purchase only to protect a tiny balance. Compare the points at risk with what you would actually spend."},{"id":"check","heading":"How to check the exact date","text":"Open Google Play with the account enrolled in Play Points. Tap the profile icon, then Play Points. Open Use and find the balance and expiry date near the top. Open Points history to confirm the latest earning or redemption activity. If the date is missing or the balance changed unexpectedly, take screenshots of the balance and history before contacting support."},{"id":"differences","heading":"Do not mix up three deadlines","text":"Your point-balance expiry, annual level progress, and the expiry of a redeemed reward are separate. Spending points reduces the balance but does not remove points already counted toward annual level progress. A Play credit or coupon received from points may still expire even while your Play Points balance remains active."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Plan a useful purchase before points expire","text":"Estimate how much spend is needed for a target number of points. Use it only for a purchase you already intend to make. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Do all points expire separately?","text":"Google shows a balance expiry date based on your latest earning or use, rather than requiring you to track each point batch separately."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Does opening Play Points reset the date?","text":"No. Viewing the page is not the same as earning or using points."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Is the expiry date the same as my level date?","text":"No. Balance expiry and calendar-year level progress are different systems."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-family-sharing.html","title":"Can Google Play Points be shared with family?","description":"Google Play Points cannot be transferred or combined with family members. Learn who earns points and how Family Library and family payments differ.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Can Google Play Points be shared with family?","text":"No. Play Points cannot be transferred, gifted, or combined between Google Accounts, even inside a Google family group. Family Library can share certain purchased content, but it does not turn individual point balances into a family balance."},{"id":"answer","heading":"What is and is not shared","text":"Item Shareable? Important detail Play Points balance No Cannot transfer or combine accounts Level progress No Tracked on the purchasing account Eligible purchased content Sometimes Family Library rules apply Family payment method Usable for eligible purchases Does not change who earns points"},{"id":"payment","heading":"Who earns points with a family payment method?","text":"The Google Account that makes the eligible purchase earns the points. Google's troubleshooting guidance says you do not earn points from purchases made by someone else in the family group, even when they use a family payment method. Before checkout, confirm the profile icon and account email. Paying with the family card does not move the points to the family manager."},{"id":"library","heading":"Family Library is different","text":"Family Library can share eligible purchased apps, games, movies, TV shows, ebooks, and audiobooks with up to five family members. Eligibility varies by content and country. In-app purchases and subscriptions generally are not the same as a shareable app purchase. A shared title remains content access; it does not share the buyer's Play Points, redeemed Play credit, coupons, or level."},{"id":"setup","heading":"A safer family setup","text":"Decide which account should own the purchase before paying. Check whether the item shows Family Library eligibility. Confirm the active Google Account at checkout. After purchase, verify points in that account's Points history. Avoid passing passwords between family members. Use Google's family features and purchase approvals instead."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Estimate points on the account that will buy","text":"Select the level of the account making the purchase. A family payment method does not merge levels or balances. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Can I send my points to a family member?","text":"No. Google does not provide point transfers or account combining, including within a family group."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Who earns points with a family payment method?","text":"The account making the eligible purchase earns the points, not automatically the family manager."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Does Family Library share Play credit?","text":"No. Family Library shares eligible content, not Play Points balances or redeemed Play credit."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-fastest-silver.html","title":"How to reach Google Play Points Silver efficiently in the US","description":"Reach the US Silver threshold of 150 points efficiently by checking progress, activating eligible offers and aligning planned purchases with the highest available earn rate.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"calculator_bridge","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"How to reach Google Play Points Silver efficiently in the US","text":"US Silver starts at 150 annual points. The strongest method is to combine your existing purchase plan with the highest eligible offer displayed on the account, rather than treating every promotion as stackable. The steps below focus on getting more Play Points value from purchases you already expect to make."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"1. Start with the exact missing points","text":"Open Play Points and read the annual progress to Silver. If you already have 90 points, plan for the remaining 60, not the full 150. Redeemable balance and annual level progress are different. Use the progress number for this calculation."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"2. Check and activate the Earn offers","text":"Open the Earn tab before checkout. Confirm the app, account, activation button, period and maximum bonus points. Use the highest eligible displayed rate. Promotions do not stack, so selecting the single correct rate produces the most reliable estimate."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"3. Align planned purchases with the best window","text":"From zero at the Bronze base rate, 150 points is about $150 before tax. At a confirmed direct rate of 2 points per $1 it is about $75; at 3 points per $1 it is about $50. These examples assume every dollar is eligible and ignore item-level rounding. Recalculate from the actual missing points and product prices."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"4. Use account benefits that are actually available","text":"The regular Friday weekly prize begins after reaching Silver, so it cannot be the main route from Bronze to Silver. An eligible Play Pass Thursday bonus or booster can contribute when it is already displayed for the account. Free or special Earn offers may appear by account. Treat the current screen and its deadline as the source of truth."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"5. Verify the result and update the plan","text":"After purchase, check Points history and subtract credited points from the remaining progress. If a bonus is pending, keep the offer terms and wait for the stated timing before recalculating. This loop—progress, offer, planned purchase, history—is more reliable than using a single fixed spending total."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Calculate with your regional settings","text":"Enter the remaining points and the confirmed earning rate shown on your account. Open the US calculator"},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"How much spending is 150 US Silver points?","text":"About $150 at the Bronze base rate before tax, starting from zero."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Can a 3-point offer reduce that estimate?","text":"Yes. If the full purchase is eligible for 3 points per $1, the zero-to-Silver estimate is about $50."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Can the regular weekly prize help Bronze reach Silver?","text":"No. The regular weekly prize starts at Silver. Play Pass and account-specific Earn offers are separate."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-gift-cards.html","title":"Can Google Play Points buy gift cards? Do gift cards earn points?","description":"Can Google Play Points buy gift cards? No. Points and Play balance cannot buy Google Play or brand gift cards. Also learn when gift-card purchases earn points.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"gift-card-answer","heading":"Can Play Points become gift cards? No—not by buying a gift card","text":"You cannot use Google Play Points or Google Play balance to buy Google Play gift cards or brand gift cards. If your Play Points “Use” tab offers Google Play credit, that becomes Play balance; it is not a gift card. Intent Answer Use Play Points to buy a gift card No. Google says Points and Play balance cannot be used to purchase Google Play or brand gift cards. Redeem Play Points for Play credit Possible when that reward is shown on your account. Play credit is balance, not a gift card. Earn Play Points by buying a digital gift card Not normally. Google says digital gift-card purchases are not eligible unless a specific promotion says otherwise. Earn points on a later eligible Play purchase paid with balance The later Google Play purchase may earn points under the enrolled account’s local rules; treat that as a separate transaction. Buying or redeeming a Google Play gift card does not normally earn Play Points by itself. After you join Play Points, an eligible Google Play purchase made with the enrolled account may earn points even when Play balance is used, subject to that country's rules and the offer shown on the account. Keep the card discount, balance redemption, and later content purchase as three separate events."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Separate discount from points","text":"A discounted gift card can lower your real cost, but the retailer's discount or card reward is not a Play Points award. Keep the saving and the Google Play earning estimate in separate columns. If either the seller reward or the Play Points eligibility is uncertain, do not combine them into one optimistic return rate."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Card purchase, redemption, and content purchase are different","text":"Buying a card from a retailer and redeeming its code into Play balance do not normally create Play Points. The later purchase of an eligible app, game, in-app item, book, movie, or subscription is the event that may earn points after the account has joined Play Points. Check the Play Points activity screen after the eligible Google Play purchase. Do not describe the card itself as the point-earning product."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Campaign combinations need care","text":"A 2x or 3x campaign may include conditions for the app, item, date, account, activation step, payment flow, or bonus cap. Using Play balance does not guarantee that every promotion applies. Calculate the purchase at the normal rate first. Use a campaign multiplier only when the offer displayed on the purchasing account clearly covers that transaction."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Compare effective cost safely","text":"Use the calculator for the eligible Google Play purchase, then record the gift-card discount separately. This keeps the earning calculation tied to the transaction that can actually produce points. A discounted card can still save money even when it does not improve status progress. Conversely, a points promotion does not automatically make a gift card the best payment choice."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Check country, currency, and planned use","text":"Gift cards are restricted by country and currency, and the account country must match. Buy only from an authorized route for the account's country and confirm that you will use the balance. Do not lock extra money into Play balance only to chase a status target. Start with a purchase you already planned, then verify the card, account, and promotion conditions."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Estimate an eligible purchase, not the card itself","text":"Use the level and earning conditions shown on the account that will make the Google Play purchase. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Can I turn Google Play Points into gift cards?","text":"No. Google says Google Play Points and Play balance cannot be used to buy Google Play or brand gift cards. A Play credit reward, when offered, is balance rather than a gift card."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Does buying or redeeming a gift card itself earn Play Points?","text":"Normally, no. Eligible purchases made later with the redeemed balance may earn points under the account's local rules."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Can I use gift-card balance with promotions?","text":"Sometimes, but the offer shown on the purchasing account must cover the transaction."},{"id":"article-section-14","heading":"Should I count gift-card savings as Play Points?","text":"No. Treat the discount as cost savings and Play Points as a separate earning estimate."},{"id":"article-section-15","heading":"Next step","text":"Points-to-cost calculator 2x promotion calculator Gift-card discount safety checklist Editorial policy"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-join-eligibility.html","title":"Can't join Google Play Points in the US? Eligibility checks","description":"Check United States Play Points availability, account management, payment method, billing address and Play country when the Join button is missing.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Short answer","text":"Google Play Points is available in the United States. If the Join button is missing, first check the account against Google's signup requirements rather than creating another payment profile or account. The quickest order is: Play country → account management and age → payment method and billing address → correct signed-in account → Google support."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"Six checks before you troubleshoot","text":"The United States is listed among Google's supported countries . You are using a compatible Android device or computer. The Google Play account is not managed by a school or a parent . You meet Google's age requirement; the current learning guide says Play Points is for users age 18 or older. The account has a valid payment method. The billing address matches the Play country."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Check the signup requirements first","text":"Google's public signup guidance combines country, device, account management, age, payment method and billing information. Passing only one of those checks is not enough. Check What should match Play country United States on the Google Play account you intend to use. Account Not managed by a school or parent; workplace accounts depend on their actual management policy. Age 18 or older under Google's current Play Points learning guidance. Payment A valid payment method with a billing address that matches the Play country. Device A supported Android device or computer."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Match the symptom to the first check","text":"What you see Check first Next action No Play Points or Join menu Play country, account management, age Confirm the affected Google account and its Play country before changing settings. Play Points appears, but signup will not complete Payment method and billing address Verify the active payment profile matches the United States. You changed Play country recently Country change status Allow for Google's stated update window before creating a second profile. You already joined, but purchase points are missing Transaction tracking Use the missing-points troubleshooting guide instead. On Android, make sure you are signed into the account that will actually make purchases, then open Profile → Play Points . Membership and balance belong to the account, not to the device as a whole."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"If you recently changed Play country","text":"Google says a Play country update can take up to 48 hours. Check the active Play country and payment profile first; do not create a second payment profile only to make the Join button appear. If you use several Google accounts on one device, confirm which account owns the payment profile and which account is open in Google Play."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Contact Google when the requirements match","text":"If the requirements match, the correct account is selected, and the relevant country-change window has passed, use Google's Play Points signup troubleshooting from the affected account. Before contacting support, note the account email, current Play country, payment profile country, device type and a screenshot of the missing menu. Keep order numbers and payment details out of public posts."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Still no Join button?","text":"Continue with Google's sign-up troubleshooting from the same account. That is the next task for this page; calculating points comes later, after membership is available. Open Google's sign-up troubleshooting"},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Is Play Points available in the United States?","text":"Yes, but the account and payment profile still need to meet the joining requirements."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Are all work accounts blocked?","text":"Google's public requirements do not state that. Managed accounts may have restrictions, so check the account administrator and Play services settings."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Is a missing Join button the same as missing purchase points?","text":"No. Join eligibility and transaction tracking are separate issues."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-level-maintenance-reset.html","title":"When does your Google Play Points level reset?","description":"Learn how long a Google Play Points level lasts, how the January review uses points earned in the previous calendar year, and why balance differs from level progress.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"calculator_bridge","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"When does your Google Play Points level reset?","text":"Your level does not simply disappear on December 31. After you reach a new level, Google says you keep it through the end of the next calendar year. At the start of each year, the level can change based on the points earned during the previous calendar year. The safest way to plan is to separate your spendable balance from your annual level progress."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"The short answer: a new level lasts through the next calendar year","text":"If you reach a new level at any point this year, Google says that level stays active until the end of the following calendar year. Reaching it in January and reaching it in December therefore produce different lengths of time, but both end at the close of the next calendar year. This is why the date shown in your own Google Play level-benefits screen matters more than a generic countdown. Country availability, level thresholds, benefits, and earning rates can differ, while the account screen shows the rule applied to you."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Balance and annual level progress are two different numbers","text":"Your Play Points balance is the amount available to exchange for eligible rewards. Level progress is the total of qualifying points earned during the current calendar year. One can go down while the other stays unchanged. A high balance does not prove that you earned enough this year to keep the same level next January. Likewise, a low balance does not necessarily mean your annual progress is low if you already spent points on rewards."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Using points does not erase level progress","text":"Google states that using or exchanging points deducts them from your Play Points balance, but it does not deduct them from level progress. You do not need to preserve the balance only to protect the points you already earned toward a level. Important exception: if you return or cancel a purchase that earned points, Google may deduct those points from both the balance and level progress. Check the points history after a refund instead of relying on an earlier screenshot. For example, reaching Gold during 2026 keeps that newly reached level through December 31, 2027. The review for January 2028 uses points earned during 2027. A zero spendable balance after redemption does not mean zero annual progress."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"What happens at the January review","text":"At the start of a calendar year, Google may change your level according to the number of points earned in the previous calendar year. If that annual total meets the requirement for your current level in your country, you can begin the new year at that level. If it does not, the account can move to the level supported by that total. Do not subtract the minimum threshold of an inherited level by yourself. For planning, use the remaining points displayed in Google Play. Local thresholds and earning rates are not universal, so another country's amount should not be copied."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Where to check before the end of the year","text":"Open the Google Play Store, tap the profile icon, open Play Points, then open More and Level benefits. Google says this screen can show the current level, benefits, base earning rate, points needed for the next level, and how long the current level remains. Also open Points history when a recent purchase, promotion, cancellation, or refund could change the annual total. Record the date and the displayed remaining points before using a calculator."},{"id":"section-6","heading":"Decide whether maintenance is worth extra spending","text":"First compare the missing points with purchases you already planned. Then run a normal-rate estimate before testing a 2x or 3x promotion. Only use a campaign multiplier when the specific purchase is eligible and the offer has been activated if required. If keeping the level requires spending far beyond your normal plan, staying at the lower level can be the better result. Weekly prizes, support, and other benefits do not have guaranteed cash value, so the level label alone should not decide the purchase."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Check the gap without forcing a purchase","text":"Enter the remaining points shown in Google Play, compare the normal rate first, and use the result as a spending limit rather than a target. Open the Platinum maintenance calculator"},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Do Google Play Points levels reset every December 31?","text":"Not as a simple full reset. A newly reached level remains through the end of the next calendar year, and the level can change at the start of a year based on points earned in the previous year."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Will redeeming points make my level go down?","text":"Redeeming points reduces the spendable balance, not the level progress already earned. A refund or cancelled purchase can deduct points from both."},{"id":"article-section-14","heading":"Which number should I enter in the calculator?","text":"Use the remaining points shown in your Google Play level screen. Do not use the spendable balance or subtract another country's threshold."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-levels.html","title":"US Google Play Points levels: requirements, rates and benefits","description":"Compare the official United States Google Play Points thresholds, earning rates, weekly prize eligibility and base spending estimates for Bronze through Diamond.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"US Google Play Points levels: requirements, rates and benefits","text":"This English guide uses the United States Play country, not Japanese, Korean or Taiwan thresholds. Silver starts at 150 annual points in the US, while the regular earning rate begins at 1 point per $1 before tax. Use the level progress shown in your own Play Points screen as the final number."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Official US level table","text":"Level Annual points Regular earning rate Bronze 0–149 1 point per $1 Silver 150–599 1.1 points per $1 Gold 600–2,999 1.2 points per $1 Platinum 3,000–9,999 1.4 points per $1 Diamond 10,000 or more 1.6 points per $1 The points shown in this table are annual level points. Your redeemable balance is a different number. Redeeming points does not reduce the annual level progress, while refunds or cancellations can remove points connected to the purchase."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Thresholds are not fixed spending totals","text":"Level thresholds are points, not fixed purchase amounts. Bronze 1 point per $1 applies while that rate is active; after reaching Silver or Gold, later purchases may earn at the new rate. Estimate from current annual progress and confirm when the new rate starts. Each item is calculated separately and rounded to the nearest whole point. The purchase preview and Points history should decide the final result."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Weekly benefits and level timing","text":"The regular weekly prize begins at Silver and resets on Friday in the Play country. Play Pass weekly bonuses and boosters are a separate Thursday benefit for eligible subscribers and family members. Level progress is based on points earned during the level period shown by Google Play. Check the app near year-end rather than assuming a lifetime total controls the next year."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"How to use the calculator","text":"Enter the points still missing, not your current redeemable balance. Use the US earning rate displayed for your level, then compare a confirmed offer only when the purchase is eligible. The US figures on this page must not be copied to another Play country. Korea and Taiwan use different thresholds, currencies and earning units."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Calculate with your regional settings","text":"Enter the remaining points and the confirmed earning rate shown on your account. Open the US calculator"},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"How many points are needed for Silver in the US?","text":"150 annual level points."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Does spending points lower my level progress?","text":"No. Redeeming points lowers the usable balance, not the annual level progress. Refunds and cancellations are different."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Is Diamond 10,000 points in every country?","text":"No. This page is for the United States Play country only."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-multiple-accounts.html","title":"Can Google Play Points be shared between accounts?","description":"Learn how Google Play Points work with multiple Google accounts, family payments, account switching, transfers, joining, and country changes.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Can Google Play Points be shared between accounts?","text":"Google Play Points belong to the enrolled Google account that makes the eligible purchase. They are not a household balance, and switching the visible account does not merge histories. Before troubleshooting missing points, identify the account that received the receipt and the account currently showing the Play Points screen."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Points stay with the enrolled purchasing account","text":"Google says points are earned for eligible purchases made from the Google account enrolled in Play Points. On a phone with several accounts, the account selected in the Play Store and the account used for payment may not be the one you expected. Compare the receipt email, Google Play order history, and Play Points profile. When those three do not match, the first task is finding the correct account rather than waiting for the visible balance to change."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Points cannot be transferred or combined","text":"Google's earning guide states that Play Points cannot be transferred between accounts or to another person. That remains true even when the accounts belong to the same user or members of the same family group. Do not plan to earn on one account and move the balance later. Choose the account deliberately before a large eligible purchase, especially when one account already has a higher level."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Family payment does not create shared points","text":"A family payment method can pay for a family member's purchase, but it does not turn the points into a shared family reward. Google's troubleshooting guide says you do not earn points when someone else in the family group makes the purchase, even if the family payment method is used. The purchaser's enrolled account is the important identity. Separate who paid from which Google account completed the purchase."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Joining later does not credit older purchases","text":"Play Points must be joined before the purchase. Google says purchases made before joining do not receive points afterward. If a new or secondary account has no Play Points history, verify whether it was enrolled at the time of purchase. Repeatedly switching accounts or joining after the receipt was issued will not recreate an earlier reward."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Account and country checks before spending","text":"Before a planned purchase, open the intended account, confirm it is enrolled, check the current level, and verify the Play country and payment profile. Country changes can remove existing points and prevent the old level from carrying over. Use the calculator only after the account is settled. A correct multiplier applied to the wrong account still produces a misleading plan."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Calculate for the account that will actually buy","text":"Enter the level and missing points shown on that account. Do not combine balances from several accounts. Open the calculator"},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Can two accounts combine points for one reward?","text":"No. Treat each account's balance and level separately."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Does the owner of a family payment method earn every family purchase?","text":"No. The eligible purchasing account matters, not only the funding source."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Which account should I use in the calculator?","text":"Use the account that will make the purchase and enter the level and missing points shown in that account."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Next step","text":"Weekly prize and reset days Points not showing after a purchase Country-change effects Platinum cost calculator Editorial policy"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-not-showing.html","title":"Google Play Points not showing up: what to check","description":"Checklist for Google Play Points not showing up, including account mismatch, eligible purchases, campaign rules, refunds, and regional differences.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"troubleshooting","sections":[{"id":"reading-answer","heading":"Check the purchase before trying again","text":"Start with the purchasing Google account, completed order and Points history. Then separate missing normal points from a missing promotional bonus. Do not make another purchase to test whether points appear. If only the bonus is missing, compare the offer’s activation, app, dates and cap with the completed purchase. The steps below explain how to narrow the cause and what to keep for official support."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"1. Confirm the Google account","text":"Open Google Play and check the account icon before anything else. Points are not pooled across accounts, so a family device, old phone, or secondary Gmail account can make the balance look wrong even when the purchase itself succeeded. If the purchase receipt is in another account, do not keep changing calculator settings to make the number match. The right next step is to review that account's Play Points activity."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"2. Check whether the purchase was eligible","text":"Buying or adding a gift card is not the same as buying eligible content with Play balance. Eligible content paid from that balance can earn points. A free trial without a charge, a purchase made before joining, and a refunded order are different cases; check which one the order actually represents. Read the purchase details and the local Play Points terms together. If the transaction is not eligible, a perfect estimate still will not create points."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"3. Separate normal points from campaign points","text":"A campaign headline such as 2x or 3x is only the front door. The actual rule can include selected apps, a date range, country availability, payment method restrictions, maximum bonus points, or a requirement to activate the offer first. If normal points appear but the bonus does not, the issue may be the campaign condition rather than a full tracking failure. That distinction makes your next action much clearer."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"4. Compare with a simple estimate","text":"Use the calculator with your current tier's normal earn rate first, then run the same amount with the final special earn rate shown for that eligible purchase in Google Play. The gap between those two results helps you explain the situation without relying on memory. The calculator is not an official account checker. Its job is to turn your assumptions into a number, then let you compare that number with Google Play's activity screen."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"5. Prepare support details only after narrowing the cause","text":"If everything still looks correct, collect the purchase date, order status, account, item name, and campaign name. Avoid posting order numbers or payment details publicly. A short, organized support message is more useful than a long explanation built from guesses."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Check the numbers without forcing a purchase","text":"Use the calculator as a planning estimate, then confirm the official rule in your local Google Play app. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Can points be transferred from another account?","text":"No. Play Points are account-based."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Does a missing bonus mean the normal points are wrong?","text":"Not necessarily. Bonus timing and regular points can be handled differently."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Should I buy again to test it?","text":"No. Do not create another purchase just to test point reflection. Confirm the existing purchase and campaign conditions first."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-platinum-diamond-cost.html","title":"US Play Points: Platinum starts at 3,000, Diamond at 10,000 annual points","description":"For US accounts, Platinum starts at 3,000 annual points and Diamond at 10,000. Compare official rates, maintenance references, shared benefits, and planned-purchase scenarios.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"calculator_bridge","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"US Play Points: Platinum starts at 3,000, Diamond at 10,000 annual points","text":"For a US Google Play account, Platinum covers 3,000–9,999 annual points and Diamond starts at 10,000. Their base rates are 1.4 and 1.6 points per $1. The right comparison is not “which badge is better,” but whether purchases you already plan put the account close enough to use the difference."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Official US thresholds and rates","text":"Item Platinum Diamond Annual points 3,000–9,999 10,000 or more Base rate 1.4 points per $1 1.6 points per $1 Weekly prizes Included Included Premium support Included Included Premium support is not Diamond-only, and weekly prizes are not guaranteed cash value. Use the benefits shown on your account as the final reference."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Maintenance references are not universal “costs to reach”","text":"If the account starts the year already at the level and every eligible purchase earns only that level's base rate, 3,000 Platinum points correspond to about $2,143 and 10,000 Diamond points to $6,250. Platinum: 3,000 ÷ 1.4 ≈ $2,143 Diamond: 10,000 ÷ 1.6 = $6,250 From a lower level, the current level's rate applies until the account advances. Taxes, item eligibility, per-purchase rounding, refunds, and promotions also change the result. Calculate from the missing annual points shown in Google Play. Do not divide every remaining point by the destination level’s rate unless the account already earns at that rate. Use the maintenance calculators for a same-level annual reference, and use the status calculator when progress still crosses lower levels."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Platinum","text":"Worth comparing when planned annual purchases already put the account near 3,000 points and you use weekly prizes or premium support."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Diamond","text":"Worth calculating when planned annual purchases already approach 10,000 points and the higher 1.6-point rate will apply repeatedly after the level is reached."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Neither as a target","text":"When the remaining amount is far above the planned Google Play budget, the current level is the more accurate financial result. An eligible special earn rate can improve the timing of a planned purchase, but it does not create a guaranteed return."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Check in this order","text":"Record the current level and annual points still needed. Confirm the base rate in the Earn tab. Calculate the normal-rate result first. Add a promotion only when the exact purchase qualifies. Compare the remaining amount with the planned annual budget."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Calculate from your account's missing points","text":"Use the current US level and the remaining annual points shown in Google Play. Open the US level calculator"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-play-credit-not-working.html","title":"Play Points credit cannot be exchanged or used: checks","description":"Check points, expiry, account, country, currency, eligible purchases, and checkout settings when Play Points credit cannot be exchanged or used.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"troubleshooting","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Play Points credit cannot be exchanged or used: checks","text":"Separate the problem into before and after exchange. If the Play credit reward cannot be selected, check required points and whether it is offered to your account. If it was already exchanged, check expiry, account, country and currency, eligible purchase, and the payment method at checkout."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Before exchange: points and reward availability","text":"Open Play Points and the Use tab to confirm that Play credit is offered on the current account. If the item is grayed out, you may not have enough points for that reward. Available rewards can differ by country, region, and account. Do not plan around a Play credit option shown only in a screenshot from another region."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"After exchange: expiry and balance","text":"Google says Play credit received from exchanging points expires after one year. Do not confuse that deadline with the expiry of the Play Points balance or other Play balance. Check the exchange or points history as well. If the exchange appears but the balance does not, confirm that checkout is using the same account that made the exchange."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Use the same account and same currency","text":"Google Play balance cannot be transferred between accounts. On a device with multiple accounts, choose the item and reach checkout with the account that holds the balance. The balance can be used for content sold in the same currency. If the country, payment profile, or item currency differs, the Play balance may not be available."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"If you changed your Play country","text":"Google says that after you changed your Play country, the old-country Play balance cannot be used in the new country. It remains tied to the previous country. A missing balance after a country change cannot be fixed by sending it to another account or cashing it out. Check the old payment profile and existing subscriptions before another purchase."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Eligible purchase and checkout method","text":"Play balance can buy or subscribe to content and services on Google Play, but it cannot buy Google Store hardware or top up Google Pay or another payment method. Country-specific availability also applies. If the balance is lower than the purchase amount, some supported purchases allow it to be combined with another payment method. Confirm Play balance is selected at checkout, and remember that Play credit exchanged from points is not refundable."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Check the condition before spending again","text":"Identify why the reward is unavailable, then estimate only the purchase you already plan to make. Open the calculator"},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Why is the Play credit reward grayed out?","text":"You may not have enough points. Check the required points and current reward availability."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"When does exchanged Play credit expire?","text":"Google says it expires after one year."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Can I use old balance after changing country?","text":"No. Old-country Play balance cannot be used in the new country."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-promotion-not-applied.html","title":"Google Play Points promotion not applied","description":"A step-by-step checklist for Google Play Points bonuses that did not apply, including activation, dates, app eligibility, caps, and normal point comparison.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"troubleshooting","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Google Play Points promotion not applied","text":"A 2x or 3x campaign looks simple in a banner, but the real rule can include activation, app, date, account, payment flow, and bonus-cap conditions. Separate missing bonus points from missing normal points before taking action."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Confirm whether the offer had to be activated","text":"Some promotions are automatic, while others require an activation button before purchase. A purchase completed before activation may not receive the bonus. Check the campaign screen and Points history on the same account that made the purchase."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Check dates, time zones, and purchase completion","text":"Campaign windows can depend on local dates, store time zones, and when a transaction completes. Pending, cancelled, refunded, or adjusted orders can change the result. Compare the receipt timestamp with the exact campaign period rather than relying on memory."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Review app, item, and payment eligibility","text":"An offer may be limited to a particular app, item or first subscription. That does not make all subscriptions or Play-balance purchases ineligible for normal points. Treat a payment restriction as an exclusion only when that offer actually states it. If normal points appear but the bonus does not, eligibility is one of the first conditions to check. As a hypothetical example, an offer for Game A with a maximum extra 100 points does not cover Game B. If the qualifying Game A purchase would produce 150 extra points before the cap, only 100 extra points are expected. This is a reading example, not a currently available offer."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Look for caps and partial bonuses","text":"Some offers have a maximum number of bonus points or apply to only part of a purchase. A smaller-than-expected bonus does not always mean the campaign failed. Confirm the normal points first, then calculate only the extra campaign portion."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Compare normal and promotional estimates","text":"Run the same purchase at the normal rate and at the verified campaign rate. The difference shows which part is actually missing. The calculator cannot prove eligibility, but it can help you prepare a clear support message with the purchase date, account, campaign, expected bonus, and actual result."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Compare the normal rate and verified promotion","text":"Use the purchasing account's local values rather than a fixed Japanese-yen example. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Does a campaign banner mean every purchase qualifies?","text":"No. Read the full offer conditions."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Can normal points appear while bonus points are missing?","text":"Yes. The two parts can have different conditions or timing."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Should I make another purchase to test it?","text":"No. Check the existing purchase and campaign first."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Next step","text":"Points-to-cost calculator 2x promotion calculator Points not showing after a purchase Editorial policy"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-promotion-stacking.html","title":"Do US Google Play Points promotions stack? Earn-rate rules","description":"Google Play Points promotions do not combine on one purchase. Learn how the highest US earn rate, activation, pre-tax price and item rounding work.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Do US Google Play Points promotions stack? Earn-rate rules","text":"For one eligible purchase, Google says multiple promotions cannot be combined. The highest available earning rate applies. A direct offer such as 3 points per $1 is not added to Silver's 1.1 points per $1."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Use the highest eligible rate","text":"If a Silver account has its regular 1.1 points per $1 rate and an eligible 3 points per $1 offer, use 3 points per $1, not 4.1. Two visible offers also do not become 5x or 6x. The checkout or offer terms decide which rate is eligible. Account-specific offers may differ even when two users open the same app page."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Read 'x' and direct-rate labels differently","text":"A label saying '3x your regular rate' is a multiplier whose base is defined by the offer. A label saying '3 points per $1' is already the final direct rate. Do not convert the two labels into the same formula without reading the terms. The Play Store preview is the safest final check."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Tax, rounding and split purchases","text":"Points are calculated from the item price excluding tax, and each item is rounded to the nearest whole point. A cart total estimate can therefore differ from the sum of item results. Splitting a purchase can change rounding, but it does not create a guaranteed advantage. Calculate the actual purchase units and verify Points history afterward."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Activation, period and caps","text":"Some offers must be activated before purchase and can limit apps, users, dates or maximum bonus points. A screenshot of an offer does not prove the current account qualifies. Use the highest eligible displayed rate in the calculator, then keep the offer terms until the points appear."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Calculate with your regional settings","text":"Enter the remaining points and the confirmed earning rate shown on your account. Open the US calculator"},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Does Silver 1.1 plus a 3-point offer become 4.1 points per $1?","text":"No. The higher eligible rate applies; the rates are not added."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Do 2x and 3x become 6x?","text":"No. Multiple promotions do not combine on one purchase."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Are taxes included in the point calculation?","text":"No. Google calculates from item price excluding tax."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-refund.html","title":"What happens to Play Points after a refund?","description":"Google Play refunds can deduct earned points from your balance and level progress. Learn about negative balances, level changes, and point-funded refunds.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"What happens to Play Points after a refund?","text":"If an eligible Google Play purchase is refunded, the points earned from that purchase are deducted from both your Play Points balance and level progress. Your balance can become negative, and a level may drop if the deduction leaves insufficient progress."},{"id":"earned","heading":"Points earned from the refunded purchase","text":"Situation What happens Purchase earned Play Points Those points are deducted from balance and level progress Points were already spent The balance may become negative Deduction crosses a level threshold Your level may go down Subscription is only canceled for the future No refund deduction unless a charged transaction is actually refunded A cancellation and a refund are not the same event. Canceling renewal generally stops a future charge; a refund reverses an existing transaction under the applicable policy."},{"id":"negative","heading":"Negative balances and level changes","text":"If you used the earned points before the refund, Google can still deduct them. The displayed balance can therefore be negative. Future earned points first offset that negative amount. Because the same points are removed from level progress, a refund can also affect your current earning rate and benefits. Check the level screen again after the refund is completed."},{"id":"spent","heading":"Refunds for items paid with points","text":"Points exchanged directly for in-app items, in-app services, Play credit, or a cause are generally not refundable. Coupon cases differ: if a purchase made with a points coupon is refunded, Google says the paid amount and the points used for the coupon can be returned, but the coupon itself is not restored. Eligibility for the purchase refund still depends on Google Play or the developer's refund policy."},{"id":"check","heading":"What to record and check","text":"Save the order number and refund confirmation. Open Play Points → Points history and note the original earned points. After the refund, compare balance and level progress. If the numbers do not update as expected, contact support with screenshots and the order number."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Recalculate after a deduction","text":"If your point balance or level changed, estimate the spend needed for your next target using the updated level. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Can my Play Points balance become negative?","text":"Yes. Google says it can become negative when a refund deduction is larger than the available balance."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Can a refund lower my Play Points level?","text":"Yes. Removing earned points from level progress may cause a level decrease."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Does canceling a subscription always remove points?","text":"Not by itself. Points are deducted when a charged purchase is returned or refunded."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-rounding-tax.html","title":"Google Play Points tax, rounding and split purchases explained","description":"See how Google Play Points exclude tax, multiply an item price by the earning rate and round to the nearest whole point, and why separate purchases can differ from one combined estimate.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-section-1","heading":"Answer: tax is excluded and the result is rounded to a whole point","text":"Google’s Play Points rules say that you earn Points from the item price, not from taxes paid. The item price is multiplied by the earning rate for your level, then the result is rounded to the nearest whole point. Because rounding happens around individual item prices, a quick estimate based only on a tax-inclusive receipt total can differ from the Points shown at checkout or in point history."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"The three-step calculation","text":"Identify the eligible item price before tax. Multiply it by the earning rate for the account’s country and level. Round the result to the nearest whole point. For a US Bronze account, the normal base rate is 1 point per $1 of eligible spending. Higher levels use a higher rate, but promotions and account-specific offers must be confirmed separately."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Example: why a half point becomes a whole number","text":"At a rate of 1.1 points per $1, an eligible $5 item produces 5.5 points before rounding. Applying the nearest-whole-point rule gives 6 points. The example explains the arithmetic; it does not guarantee that every $5 purchase is eligible. If the displayed price includes tax, do not automatically multiply the final charged amount. Use the estimated Points shown before purchase whenever Google provides them."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Separate purchases and one combined estimate are not the same","text":"Suppose two eligible items each calculate to 0.6 point. Rounding each purchase separately produces 1 point plus 1 point, for 2 points. Adding the two prices first could produce 1.2 points and then round to only 1 point. This comparison shows why a monthly total divided by the base unit is only an estimate. Do not split purchases solely to chase rounding; eligibility, tax, promotions and refunds matter more than a small theoretical difference."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"What can make the final number different?","text":"The item or payment is not eligible for Play Points. The displayed price includes tax that does not earn Points. A promotion applies only after activation or only to selected items. The purchase uses another Google account or Play country. A refund later removes the Points earned from the transaction. The safest order is checkout estimate first, completed order second and point history third. A calculator should help explain the rule, not override those account-specific records."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"How is rounding calculated for Play Points?","text":"Points are calculated per transaction and rounded to the nearest whole integer based on regional rules."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Does tax count towards earning Play Points?","text":"In most regions including the US, points are earned on the pre-tax item price only."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-subscriptions.html","title":"Do subscriptions earn Google Play Points?","description":"Eligible subscriptions billed through Google Play can earn Play Points. Learn how renewals, trials, refunds, and promotions vary by account and country.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"reference","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Do subscriptions earn Google Play Points?","text":"Yes. Eligible subscriptions billed through Google Play can earn Play Points after the purchasing account joins the program. The specific subscription, earning rate, renewal treatment, and promotion can differ by country and account. Estimate only completed eligible charges, not a free trial or an annual total you have not decided to pay."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Eligible Google Play subscription charges","text":"Google includes eligible subscriptions among the Google Play purchases that can earn Play Points. The charge must be made through Google Play with the enrolled account and must be eligible under that account's country rules. A subscription paid outside Google Play or a service not shown as eligible should not be included in the estimate."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Separate the first charge from renewals","text":"A subscription can have an initial charge, trial conversion, introductory price, and recurring renewal. Treat each completed transaction separately rather than assuming the first result applies forever. For a near-term decision, use the next renewal amount. Annualize only when you genuinely intend to keep and pay for the full period."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"Check trials, discounts, cancellations, and refunds","text":"A free trial without an eligible charge does not create point-earning spend. Introductory prices, grace periods, partial adjustments, cancellations, and refunds can also change the final point result. Use the receipt and Play Points history after the charge settles. If a charge is refunded, the points and level progress from that purchase can be deducted."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Promotions may not cover every renewal","text":"A 2x or 3x offer may cover selected apps, new subscriptions, first payments, or transactions completed during a limited window. A later renewal may earn only the normal rate. Calculate at the normal rate first, then apply a promotion only to a charge clearly covered by the offer shown on the purchasing account."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Use recurring spend as a pace check","text":"Normal subscription spending can help estimate progress toward a level without inventing new purchases. Add only the eligible charges you already plan to keep. If the remaining level cost is still much larger than your normal subscriptions, do not add unnecessary services only for status."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Estimate only subscriptions you already plan to keep","text":"Use the purchasing account's current level and the eligible charge shown in Google Play. Open the points-cost calculator"},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Can subscriptions earn Google Play Points?","text":"Yes. Eligible subscriptions billed through Google Play can earn points after the account joins Play Points."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Do all subscription renewals earn the same amount?","text":"No. The eligible amount, rate, promotion, and refund status can differ for each charge."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Can a free trial earn points?","text":"Do not count a trial with no eligible charge. Check the actual billed transaction and Points history."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Next step","text":"Points-to-cost calculator 2x promotion calculator What a refund does to Play Points Editorial policy"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-super-weekly-reward.html","title":"Google Play Points Super Weekly Prize: eligibility, prizes and Super Tickets","description":"Check Super Weekly Prize eligibility and limited stock. Super Ticket rules must be confirmed on the current account card; older ticket schedules are not verified current terms.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"retention","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Google Play Points Super Weekly Prize: eligibility, prizes and Super Tickets","text":"Use this guide only when the Super Weekly Prize card is currently shown for your Play country and Google account. Google lists Gold, Platinum and Diamond members for the promotion, but availability and extra requirements can still differ by region and account. The card on your own Perks screen is the practical starting point."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"How it differs from the regular weekly prize","text":"The names are similar, but they are separate benefits. Google's regular weekly prize starts at Silver. The Super Weekly Prize promotion lists Gold, Platinum and Diamond and may add account-specific eligibility. Play Pass weekly bonuses are another program that refreshes on Thursday in supported regions. Before troubleshooting, identify which screen you expected: the regular weekly prize, the Super Weekly Prize, or a Play Pass weekly bonus."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Eligibility, timing and limited stock","text":"When the promotion is available, Google says new prizes become available after 12:00 a.m. local time on Friday. Prize sets can differ by level, and quantities can run out. A qualifying level alone does not prove that the promotion is active for every country or account. Read the card shown in Google Play for the current eligibility, remaining stock and terms. Gold, Platinum and Diamond are the levels listed on the promotion. Additional requirements may be displayed for an individual account. Prize quantities are limited and some rewards are rarer than others. Past hardware, gift card and points examples describe the format, not this week's inventory. For purchase planning, treat the reward as an uncertain benefit rather than adding a fixed prize value to the level."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"How to claim it without mixing up accounts","text":"Open Google Play with the account enrolled in Play Points. Confirm the current level and Play country. Open Play Points and the Perks area after the local Friday refresh. Read the displayed eligibility, prize stock and additional terms. Claim the reward, then use Points history if you are unsure whether it completed. With several Google accounts, check the account icon before comparing the level and reward screen. A level on one account does not unlock a card on another."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Super Tickets: compare before using one","text":"Older Google material described a ticket for trying again after a points prize. The linked page is not currently available, so that description is historical rather than a verified current rule. Before using a ticket, read the current card for eligibility, expiry, whether the first prize is replaced, and how another ticket is obtained. If those terms are not shown, do not spend to chase a ticket."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"Read the replacement terms before deciding","text":"A useful first reward is a reason to read the replacement terms carefully. Compare only the conditions visible on your account, not a past 30-day or 90-day schedule."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"What to check when it does not appear","text":"The Google account enrolled in Play Points The current level and Play country Whether the local Friday refresh has passed Whether this week's reward was already claimed Any additional eligibility or out-of-stock message Whether you were looking for the regular or Play Pass reward instead This site cannot see live eligibility, ticket inventory or prize stock. When the card is displayed but does not work as described, keep a private screenshot of its terms and contact Google Play support without posting account or order details publicly."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Can Silver members claim the Super Weekly Prize?","text":"The Super Weekly Prize promotion lists Gold, Platinum and Diamond. Silver members may still have the separate regular weekly prize."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Does claiming early guarantee a better reward?","text":"No. Limited stock can change, but no particular premium item is guaranteed."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"How should I include this perk in a purchase decision?","text":"Start with purchases you already plan to make and compare the level benefits separately. Because prize probabilities and future stock are not published, the Super Weekly Prize should not be counted as guaranteed monetary value."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Compare planned purchases first","text":"Use the calculator for the purchase amount and level progress you can verify. Add the weekly prize only as a possible account-specific benefit. Open the calculator"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-use-coupons.html","title":"Best ways to use Google Play Points: coupons, credit and rewards","description":"What can you buy with Google Play Points? Compare coupons, Play credit, in-app items and partner rewards by real value, restrictions, expiry and refund rules.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"decision_support","sections":[{"id":"quick-answer","heading":"Short answer: use points on something you were already going to buy","text":"There is no single best Play Points reward for everyone. A coupon can be excellent when it matches a purchase you already planned, but poor value if it makes you spend extra. Play credit is more flexible when it appears on your account, while in-app items and partner vouchers can be worthwhile only when you actually want the specific reward. Open Google Play → Play Points → Use , then compare the reward shown on your account by required points, real value to you, minimum purchase, expiry and refund rules."},{"id":"article-section-2","heading":"Choose by real value, not the biggest displayed number","text":"Reward Best when Check before redeeming How to judge value App or game coupon You already plan to buy in that app or game Eligible app, minimum purchase, discount and expiry Discount you will actually use ÷ points required Google Play credit You want more flexibility about what to buy Credit amount, eligible purchases and one-year expiry Credit you expect to use ÷ points required In-app or in-game item You genuinely want that exact item Destination app/game and no-refund rule What you would personally have paid for it ÷ points required Partner voucher You already use the participating partner Redemption deadline and partner-specific terms Savings you will realistically use ÷ points required A reward that looks like “$10 value” is not worth $10 to you if you would never have bought it. The useful comparison is value you would actually use ÷ points required ."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Start with the rewards on your own account","text":"Open Google Play with the account that owns the points. Open Play Points, then Use . Shortlist only rewards you are realistically likely to use. For each one, note the required points, discount or credit amount, minimum purchase and expiry. Redeem only after the reward beats your other realistic options. Reward availability can vary by country, account and time. An old screenshot from another account is not a reliable list of what you can redeem today."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Coupon vs Google Play credit: which is better?","text":"Question Coupon Play credit Where can it be used? Only the named eligible app/game purchase and conditions Eligible Google Play purchases supported by Play balance Minimum purchase? Often part of the coupon conditions Depends on the purchase and balance rules, not an app-specific coupon minimum Expiry after points exchange? One year One year Best fit A near-term purchase you already planned You want to keep the eventual purchase more flexible A coupon is not automatically better because its headline discount is larger. When the purchase qualifies, a redeemed coupon can apply automatically at checkout; still confirm the applied discount before paying. If meeting the minimum purchase makes you spend money you would not otherwise spend, subtract that extra spending from the practical benefit. ( Google’s official guidance ) For example, suppose the same points could buy $1 of Play credit or a $2 coupon requiring a $5 purchase. On an already planned eligible $5 purchase, the coupon leaves $3 to pay, versus $4 with the credit. Without that purchase plan, using the coupon creates a $3 expense. These are hypothetical US amounts, not a live offer or exchange rate."},{"id":"article-section-6","heading":"In-app items and partner vouchers need a different check","text":"An in-app or in-game item is useful only inside the specific app or game. Google says points used for in-app or in-game items, or to support a cause, cannot be refunded. Confirm the destination before redeeming. Partner vouchers, when offered, are saved under the Play Points Perks area and can have their own redemption deadline and partner conditions. Do not value a partner voucher at face value unless you would actually use that partner."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Where each reward goes after you redeem points","text":"Reward Where to look If it seems missing Coupon Play Points → More → Points history; also Notifications & offers → Saved offers Check the same Google account and the coupon conditions Play credit Your Google Play balance / payment methods Check the account, country/currency and expiry In-app item Inside the relevant app or game Reopen the app and confirm the linked account Partner voucher Play Points → Perks Check the voucher's displayed deadline and terms If a coupon or Play credit exists but will not apply at checkout, use the dedicated troubleshooting guides instead of redeeming the same reward again."},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"When you should not redeem yet","text":"You do not know what you would use the reward for. The coupon requires an extra purchase you were not planning. You are choosing an item only because its displayed price looks high. The reward will likely expire before you use it. You have not confirmed which Google account or Play country owns the points. Keeping the points can be better than forcing a redemption. Also check the expiry of your Play Points balance separately from the expiry of a reward after redemption."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Estimate the points you still need","text":"After choosing a reward you would actually use, estimate the spending needed to earn any remaining points with the rate shown on your account. Open the calculator"},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"What is the best way to use Google Play Points?","text":"Usually, choose the reward that replaces spending you already planned. Compare required points, real value to you, minimum purchase, expiry and refund limits."},{"id":"article-section-13","heading":"Are coupons always better than Google Play credit?","text":"No. A coupon can be better for a specific planned purchase, while Play credit can preserve more flexibility. Compare the actual options shown on your account."},{"id":"article-section-14","heading":"Do coupons and Play credit expire?","text":"Google says coupons and Play credit received by exchanging Play Points expire after one year."},{"id":"article-section-15","heading":"Can I undo an in-app item exchange?","text":"Google says points used for in-app or in-game items or donations cannot be refunded."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-points-weekly-reward.html","title":"US Google Play Points weekly rewards: Friday and Thursday","description":"Understand the US regular weekly prize for Silver and above, the separate Thursday Play Pass bonus or booster, and how to handle Super Weekly displays.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"retention","sections":[{"id":"weekly-answer","heading":"Start with the name of the benefit","text":"The regular Play Points weekly prize is for Silver level or above and resets Friday. Play Pass weekly bonuses and boosters are separate Thursday benefits for eligible subscribers and family members. Thursday and Friday refer to different benefits , so seeing one does not confirm that the other is missing. For Super Weekly, use the eligibility and claim conditions on the card shown in your own Perks tab. Which weekly benefit are you checking? Benefit Where to start What to check Regular weekly prize Play Points → Perks Silver or above, Friday reset, claim history Play Pass weekly benefit The Play Pass benefit screen Subscription or family eligibility, Thursday update, expiry Super Weekly The card shown to your account Eligibility, available stock and the claim window"},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Regular weekly prize: Silver and above","text":"Open Google Play > Profile > Play Points > Perks. The regular weekly prize starts at Silver under the current US level rules and resets on Friday in the Play country. ( Google’s official guidance ) The exact reward can differ by level and account. Another person's screenshot is not a guaranteed prize table for your account."},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Play Pass Thursday benefit is separate","text":"Eligible Play Pass subscribers and eligible family members can receive a weekly Play Points bonus or booster that refreshes Thursday. It is not the same as the regular Friday prize. An unclaimed Play Pass benefit can be replaced by the next week's benefit, so use the date shown in the Play Pass screen."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"How to interpret Super Weekly","text":"Google has referenced Super Weekly rewards in system updates, but there is no stable public table that guarantees the feature for every US account. If it appears in Perks, follow the eligibility, stock and claim window shown there. Do not infer eligibility from a Korean, Taiwan or Japanese screenshot. Feature rollout and prize stock can be account-specific."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Troubleshooting the correct benefit","text":"For the Friday prize, check level and the Perks tab. For the Thursday benefit, check Play Pass subscription and family eligibility. For Super Weekly, check the exact card displayed in the account. Points history confirms a credited reward more reliably than reopening an animation. For example, if you claimed a Play Pass benefit on Thursday and see another card on Friday, the two cards are not necessarily duplicates. Read their names before comparing the rewards. Conversely, a missing Friday card is not explained simply by having claimed Thursday’s benefit. If the expected card is absent, first confirm the signed-in account and benefit type, then check the claim history. A recorded claim and an unrecorded claim lead to different questions: whether the reward was credited, or whether you were eligible to claim it. Keep the relevant date and screen details for support; repeated purchases are not a useful way to test reward eligibility. When keeping a weekly record, use the points actually credited in Points history. If the reward is a voucher or another prize, record its name as a prize instead of treating its face value as earned points. This keeps your reward diary useful when comparing weeks with different types of benefits."},{"id":"article-section-7","heading":"Calculate with your regional settings","text":"Enter the remaining points and the confirmed earning rate shown on your account. Open the US calculator"},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Can Bronze claim the regular weekly prize?","text":"No. The regular weekly prize begins at Silver under the current US rules."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Why does one reward refresh Thursday and another Friday?","text":"Thursday is the separate Play Pass weekly benefit; Friday is the regular Play Points weekly prize."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Is Super Weekly guaranteed for every US account?","text":"No public rule guarantees that. Use the Perks screen shown on the account."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/google-play-quests.html","title":"Google Play quests: why they do not appear or complete","description":"Google Play quest not showing or not completing? Check eligibility, purchase rules, Play Games sign-in, personalization, refunds and stuck progress before spending again.","category":"","tags":[],"role":"retention","sections":[{"id":"quest-quick-check","heading":"Quest missing or stuck? Start with the type of failure","text":"What you see Check first The quest never appears Eligibility is account-specific; another user seeing it does not mean your account should. A purchase step does not count The transaction must earn Play Points; redeeming points or paying with points does not count as a quest purchase. A game/play step stays incomplete Check the Google account, Play Games profile and any Play personalization requirement shown by the quest. A completed quest becomes incomplete A canceled or refunded qualifying purchase can remove progress and may reverse a claimed reward. Progress is still missing after the stated processing time Save the quest terms, order time and account used before contacting Google Play support. Do not make another purchase just to test the quest until you know which condition failed. The quest progress page on your own account is the final rule for steps, deadline and reward. A Google Play quest is a set of steps that awards a benefit only after all required steps are complete. The offer, deadline and task list can differ by account. Use your own quest progress page as the final rule instead of copying another user's purchase amount or play condition."},{"id":"section-1","heading":"Where quests appear and why yours may be missing","text":"Eligible users may see a quest on the Play Points page or in Play Store tabs such as Games, Apps or You. Not seeing an offer that appears on somebody else's account is not, by itself, evidence of an error. Google says the quests you receive can depend on account settings or activity, including Play Points level where relevant. The steps and rewards may also differ between users. ( Google’s official guidance )"},{"id":"section-2","heading":"Five details to save before you start","text":"Deadline: check both the completion deadline and any reward claim deadline. Account: confirm the Google account that opened or started the quest. Exact steps: save the app, game, amount, count and play requirements. Order: steps are normally flexible unless the quest page says otherwise. Settings: note whether Play personalization or a Play Games profile is required. A screenshot before purchase is more useful than trying to reconstruct the terms after progress fails. Keep it private because account names and offer details may be visible. For a hypothetical US offer requiring two purchases of at least $5 each, one $10 purchase would still be only one purchase. A completed purchase step would not finish a separate play-time step either. This is an example of how to read a task, not a currently available offer; use the amounts and task count on your own screen."},{"id":"section-3","heading":"What counts as a purchase","text":"For a Play Points reward quest, Google defines a purchase as a Google Play transaction that earns Play Points. Depending on the country and offer, this can include: paid apps or games; in-app or in-game items; subscription payments and renewals; book or movie rentals that earn points in the region. Redeeming points for an item, or paying with points, does not count. Those actions do not generate new Play Points. An extended trial without a payment method may also earn no points and therefore fail the purchase step."},{"id":"section-4","heading":"Play Games sign-in and personalization","text":"Some game-related quests require Play personalization so Google Play can track in-game activity. Some also require the game session to use a signed-in Play Games profile. Check the Google account used for the quest. Check the account signed in to the game and Play Games profile. Read the quest page for a personalization requirement. Confirm the required play time, achievement or task is actually complete. Reopen the progress page and look for a stated processing period. Do not turn on additional tracking just because a third-party guide says so. Read the condition on your quest and make your own privacy choice."},{"id":"section-5","heading":"Cancellations, refunds and missing progress","text":"If a qualifying purchase is canceled while the quest is incomplete, Google says you can make another qualifying purchase while the offer remains active. If a purchase is refunded after completion, the quest can return to incomplete. A claimed quest reward may be reversed when the purchase affecting eligibility is reversed. An optional bonus task may behave differently and might not return to incomplete. Pending or canceled orders should be checked before assuming a tracking failure. When progress still does not update, record the quest name, deadline, step, order time and the account used. Contact Google Play support after the stated processing time. Never publish an order number or email address in a forum."},{"id":"article-section-9","heading":"Can I complete the steps in any order?","text":"Usually yes, unless the quest progress page gives a specific order."},{"id":"article-section-10","heading":"Does a Play Pass renewal count?","text":"Google lists a Play Pass subscription renewal as a purchase when it earns Play Points and meets the quest terms."},{"id":"article-section-11","heading":"Why did my completed quest become incomplete?","text":"A canceled or refunded qualifying purchase can remove the progress that completed the quest."},{"id":"article-section-12","heading":"Only calculate spending after confirming the task","text":"If the quest genuinely requires a purchase you already planned, use the calculator to estimate ordinary points. Do not buy first and search for the quest later. Open the calculator"}]},{"path":"/en/articles/heaven-burns-red-google-play-vs-web-shop.html","title":"Heaven Burns Red Web Shop vs Google Play: a region-aware buying guide","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. Heaven Burns Red operates localized Web Shops in some Asian markets. The Taiwan shop currently lists NT$ Quartz packs and shop points and explicit","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Heaven Burns Red Web Shop vs Google Play: a region-aware buying guide","text":"Heaven Burns Red operates localized Web Shops in some Asian markets. The Taiwan shop currently lists NT$ Quartz packs and shop points and explicitly limits purchases to Taiwan, Hong Kong and Macao. That does not create a matching US shop. Which route makes sense? Use US Play Points only when your version is billed through Google Play."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"If an official Web Shop is supported for your region, compare its own current bonus separately. Do not convert Taiwan/Japan Web Shop pricing into a US offer."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Open the official site for the game region you actually use. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Can US players treat the Taiwan WEBSHOP as a US store?","text":"No. The Taiwan shop explicitly limits its purchase region."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/honkai-impact-3rd-google-play-vs-top-up-center.html","title":"Honkai Impact 3rd Google Play vs Top-Up Center: Crystals and Monthly Card","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. HoYoverse operates an official Top-Up Center for Honkai Impact 3rd, with payment methods and currencies varying by country/region. It is an offici","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Honkai Impact 3rd Google Play vs Top-Up Center: Crystals and Monthly Card","text":"HoYoverse operates an official Top-Up Center for Honkai Impact 3rd, with payment methods and currencies varying by country/region. It is an official but separate checkout from Google Play. Which route makes sense? Compare Top-Up Center bonuses with Play Points as separate benefit columns."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Do not add US Play Points to an external HoYoverse checkout. Check Monthly Card validity before adding more time."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Confirm server, HoYoverse account, country and currency. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Does official Top-Up Center mean Play Points too?","text":"No. Official publisher checkout is not automatically Google Play billing."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/honkai-star-rail-express-supply-pass.html","title":"Express Supply Pass value: Stellar Jade and Google Play Points","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. The Express Supply Pass is a time-based purchase; daily Stellar Jade claims matter. Treat immediate Oneiric Shards/top-ups separately from the del","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Express Supply Pass value: Stellar Jade and Google Play Points","text":"The Express Supply Pass is a time-based purchase; daily Stellar Jade claims matter. Treat immediate Oneiric Shards/top-ups separately from the delayed daily value. Which route makes sense? Use actual claimed days when valuing the pass."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Compare immediate top-ups separately. Only eligible Google Play billing earns Play Points."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Use the live local price and current payment route. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Does the pass deliver all value immediately?","text":"No. A major part is received through daily login rewards."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/jujutsu-kaisen-phantom-parade-google-play-vs-web-shop.html","title":"Jujutsu Kaisen Phantom Parade Web Shop vs Google Play: Japan-version guide","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. The Japanese official Web Shop is active and sells Web-exclusive Kaiju packs, passes and shop miles in yen. This is a Japan-service storefront; it","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Jujutsu Kaisen Phantom Parade Web Shop vs Google Play: Japan-version guide","text":"The Japanese official Web Shop is active and sells Web-exclusive Kaiju packs, passes and shop miles in yen. This is a Japan-service storefront; its catalog should not be currency-converted into a US/global official offer. Which route makes sense? Japanese-version users can compare the Web Shop extra currency/miles with that account's in-app route."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Do not count US Play Points on the Japanese Web Shop transaction. A global version must use its own official store and currency."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Confirm game version, account linking and age-verification requirements. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Is the Japanese Web Shop a US/global store?","text":"No. It is a Japanese-service storefront."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/monster-strike-google-play-vs-web-shop.html","title":"Monster Strike Google Play vs Web Shop: a region-aware comparison","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. Monster Strike Web Shop offers are market-specific. Taiwan's active official shop publishes local Orb bonuses and prices, but that does not create","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Monster Strike Google Play vs Web Shop: a region-aware comparison","text":"Monster Strike Web Shop offers are market-specific. Taiwan's active official shop publishes local Orb bonuses and prices, but that does not create a matching US shop or US Orb catalog. Which route makes sense? Use the purchase route officially supported by your game version."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Count US Play Points only for eligible Google Play billing. Do not currency-convert a Japan/Taiwan store into a fake US price list."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Publisher shop bonuses and Play Points are separate benefits. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Is there one global Monster Strike Web Shop price list?","text":"No. Official offers and supported markets differ."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/pokemon-go-google-play-vs-web-store.html","title":"Pokémon GO Google Play vs Web Store: PokéCoins, Reward Road and Play Points","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. Niantic operates the official Pokémon GO Web Store, where bundles and PokéCoin offers can differ from the app. Reward Road is offered only under i","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Pokémon GO Google Play vs Web Store: PokéCoins, Reward Road and Play Points","text":"Niantic operates the official Pokémon GO Web Store, where bundles and PokéCoin offers can differ from the app. Reward Road is offered only under its current regional/account conditions and is separate from Google Play Points. Which route makes sense? Use Google Play when the live Play Points rate is more valuable."},{"id":"article-section-3","heading":"Which route makes sense?","text":"Use the Web Store when its extra PokéCoins, bundle or Reward Road progress is stronger. Do not count one purchase as both Web Store and Google Play billing."},{"id":"article-section-4","heading":"Before you pay","text":"Check whether Reward Road is currently available to your region/account. Recheck the live price, supported country, account and checkout provider immediately before payment."},{"id":"article-section-5","heading":"Check the purchase in the game calculator","text":"Use the amount and final earn rate shown on the Google Play account that will actually pay. Open game calculator"},{"id":"article-section-8","heading":"Do Web Store purchases earn Google Play Points?","text":"Not as Google Play purchases; the Web Store has its own offers and programs."}]},{"path":"/en/articles/pokemon-tcg-pocket-premium-pass.html","title":"Pokémon TCG Pocket Premium Pass: 14-day trial, renewal and Play Points","description":"Region-aware game purchase guide. Pokémon Support states that Premium Pass is valid for one month, its no-cost trial lasts 14 days, and that trial is available once per Apple Accou","category":"","tags":[],"role":"game_decision","sections":[{"id":"article-title","heading":"Pokémon TCG Pocket Premium Pass: 14-day trial, renewal and Play Points","text":"Pokémon Support states that Prem